{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01STQMnfNt5dongkRbPD6tXz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012E1WitpW7NLWrGRb4S6HGk","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. Produce a detailed, independent review. Do NOT suggest an overall score yet — that comes later in the roundtable.\n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal; 9=strong accept top 15%; 8=clear accept top 50%; 7=good paper accept; 6=marginally above threshold; 5=marginally below; 4=not good enough reject; 3=clear reject; 2=strong reject; 1=trivial/wrong)\n- Acceptance rate: ~30-35%\n- Key criteria: significance/novelty of results (theoretical or empirical), objective establishment of results, potential for scientific/technological impact, relevance to robot learning\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic Routing of LoRA Experts for Parameter-Efficient VLA Robustness\"\n\nThe paper proposes Meta-Brain, a modular framework for improving VLA robustness under environmental perturbations. Key idea: VLA internal representations already encode perturbation type (linear probe on PaliGemma layer 6 achieves F1=0.993). A lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR, 48K params) diagnoses perturbation type and routes to one of three type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual, Spatial, Physical). On LIBERO-Plus, Meta-Brain achieves 78.4% with 6.1M params, +24.8% over frozen baseline, +13.6% over universal LoRA (p&lt;0.001). With optional Correction Memory (CM), reaches 80.8%, closing 90% of gap to VLA-GSE (81.2%, 114M params) at 19x fewer params. Cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA confirms generalizability (+11.8% over universal LoRA).\n\nThree claims:\n1. Perturbation diagnosability (F1=0.993 from single layer)\n2. Targeted > uniform correction (+13.6%, p&lt;0.001)\n3. Parameter-efficient robustness (19x fewer params vs VLA-GSE)\n\n## Paper Structure (9 pages, 13 tables)\n- Section 1: Introduction — three claims, contributions\n- Section 2: Related Work — VLA robustness (LIBERO-Plus, SAFE, VLA-GSE, RobustVLA), parameter-efficient adaptation (LoRA, MoIRA, CORAL, LoRA-SP, FTM/FLA)\n- Section 3: Method\n  - 3.1 Phase 0: Perturbation Diagnosis (linear probe on PaliGemma layer 6, 3-class F1=0.993)\n  - 3.2 Phase 1: Meta-Brain Architecture (PAR 48K params, ALB 3 frozen LoRA experts rank-16, CM optional with FAISS)\n  - Training: LoRA experts trained independently on type-specific data then frozen; only PAR fine-tuned E2E\n- Section 4: Experiments\n  - 4.1 Setup: LIBERO-Plus, 6 perturbation types, pi0 backbone, 5 seeds\n  - 4.2 Expert Specialization (Table 1: cross-eval matrix, Table 2: specialization delta +12-20%)\n  - 4.3 Ablation Ladder (Table 3: Frozen 53.6 → Universal 64.8 → Meta-Brain 78.4 → Oracle 79.6; Table 4: per-perturbation)\n  - 4.4 Oracle Routing (Table 5: PAR-Oracle gap 1.2%)\n  - 4.5 FTM Comparison (Table 6: FTM 68.3% overall, visual-only; Meta-Brain +10.1%)\n  - 4.6 Parameter Efficiency (Table 7: gain/M-param comparison; VLA-GSE on different backbone*)\n  - 4.7 CM Ablation (Table 8: +2.4% gain, noise +4.0%)\n  - 4.8 Router Architecture (Table 9: Linear 76.6, MLP 78.4, Attention 78.8)\n  - 4.9 6-class vs 3-class (Table 10: +1.0% at 2x params, robot -4.0%)\n  - 4.10 Cross-backbone OpenVLA (Table 11: gain over universal +11.8%, F1=0.972)\n  - 4.11 Layer Placement (Tables 12-13: V-early, S-mid, P-late diagonal; type-aligned +1.8%)\n- Section 5: Discussion — routing > capacity argument, easy diagnosis ≠ trivial, limitations\n- Section 6: Conclusion\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (review_context.yaml)\nThe imagined experiment plan includes these experiments:\n1. plan_002: LoRA expert training (3 specialized + 1 universal) → Table 1-2\n2. plan_003: Meta-Brain assembly PAR+ALB E2E → Tables 3-4\n3. plan_004: Oracle routing ablation → Table 5\n4. plan_005: Ablation ladder 5 seeds → Table 3\n5. plan_006: FTM/FLA reproduction → Table 6\n6. plan_007: Parameter efficiency analysis → Table 7\n7. plan_008: Correction Memory ablation → Table 8\n8. plan_009: Router architecture comparison → Table 9\n9. plan_010: 6-class vs 3-class → Table 10\n10. plan_011: Cross-backbone OpenVLA → Table 11\n11. plan_012: LoRA layer placement → Tables 12-13\n\nAll imagined experiments appear to be covered in the paper.\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Method soundness**: Is the Phase 0 → Phase 1 pipeline technically sound? Is the PAR router well-motivated? Is the soft routing formulation appropriate? Is the CM module well-designed?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are the experiments well-designed? Are baselines fair? Is the statistical methodology appropriate (5 seeds, paired t-tests)? Are there confounds?\n3. **Result interpretation**: Are claims supported by the evidence? Is the ablation ladder convincing? Is the parameter efficiency comparison fair (VLA-GSE uses different backbone)?\n4. **Missing experiments / controls**: What experiments are missing that would strengthen the paper?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters specified?\n\nStructure your review as:\n### Summary\n### Strengths (S1, S2, ...)\n### Weaknesses (W1, W2, ...)\n### Questions for Authors (Q1, Q2, ...)\n### Missing Experiments\n### Minor Issues\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Identify both genuine weaknesses and genuine strengths."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":692,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1752,"cache_read_input_tokens":67582,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1752},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f5e0932-32f2-4b4b-9817-0d2ade2fda2a","uuid":"c1df3057-66ea-4dfb-ac25-de226cb06480"}
