niques (probing + MoE routing + LoRA)? How does it compare to the rich MoE/routing literature?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are there important missing references? The paper mentions MoIRA, CORAL, LoRA-SP without citations. Are there other relevant works in:\n   - Mixture of LoRA experts (MoLoRA, LoRAMoE, etc.)\n   - Failure detection/diagnosis in robotics\n   - Domain-specific adaptation / domain routing\n   - Test-time adaptation for robustness\n   - Robust visual policy transfer beyond FTM\n3. **Positioning and differentiation**: Does the paper adequately distinguish itself from SAFE (which also probes VLA internals for perturbation detection) and VLA-GSE (which also uses MoE routing)?\n4. **Contribution significance**: Are the three claimed contributions truly distinct, or is this one contribution (the system) presented as three?\n5. **Broader impact and limitations**: Are limitations honestly discussed? Is the simulation-only evaluation adequate for CoRL?\n6. **Writing quality and clarity**: Is the paper well-written? Is the narrative coherent?\n\nStructure your review as:\n### Summary\n### Strengths (S1, S2, ...)\n### Weaknesses (W1, W2, ...)\n### Questions for Authors (Q1, Q2, ...)\n### Missing References\n### Minor Issues\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Identify both genuine weaknesses and genuine strengths.","uuid":"d9bdd620-2b8d-4e42-86db-b7f274f8cbc8","session_id":"1f5e0932-32f2-4b4b-9817-0d2ade2fda2a"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**. Produce a detailed, independent review. Do NOT suggest an overall score yet — that comes later in the roundtable.\n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal; 9=strong accept top 15%; 8=clear accept top 50%; 7=good paper accept; 6=marginally above threshold; 5=marginally below; 4=not good enough reject; 3=clear reject; 2=strong reject; 1=trivial/wrong)\n- Acceptance rate: ~30-35%\n- Key criteria: significance/novelty of results (theoretical or empirical), objective establishment of results, potential for scientific/technological impact, relevance to robot learning\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic Routing of LoRA Experts for Parameter-Efficient VLA Robustness\"\n\nThe paper proposes Meta-Brain, a modular framework for improving VLA robustness under environmental perturbations. Key idea: VLA internal representations already encode perturbation type (linear probe on PaliGemma layer 6 achieves F1=0.993). A lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR, 48K params) diagnoses perturbation type and routes to one of three type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual, Spatial, Physical). On LIBERO-Plus, Meta-Brain achieves 78.4% with 6.1M params, +24.8% over frozen baseline, +13.6% over universal LoRA (p&lt;0.001). With optional Correction Memory (CM), reaches 80.8%, closing 90% of gap to VLA-GSE (81.2%, 114M params) at 19x fewer params. Cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA confirms generalizability (+11.8% over universal LoRA).\n\nThree claims:\n1. Perturbation diagnosability (F1=0.993 from single layer)\n2. Targeted > uniform correction (+13.6%, p&lt;0.001)\n3. Parameter-efficient robustness (19x fewer params vs VLA-GSE)\n\n## Paper Structure (9 pages, 13 tables)\n- Section 1: Introduction — three claims, contributions\n- Section 2: Related Work — VLA robustness (LIBERO-Plus, SAFE, VLA-GSE, RobustVLA), parameter-efficient adaptation (LoRA, MoIRA, CORAL, LoRA-SP, FTM/FLA)\n- Section 3: Method\n  - 3.1 Phase 0: Perturbation Diagnosis (linear probe on PaliGemma layer 6, 3-class F1=0.993)\n  - 3.2 Phase 1: Meta-Brain Architecture (PAR 48K params, ALB 3 frozen LoRA experts rank-16, CM optional with FAISS)\n  - Training: LoRA experts trained independently on type-specific data then frozen; only PAR fine-tuned E2E\n- Section 4: Experiments\n  - 4.1 Setup: LIBERO-Plus, 6 perturbation types, pi0 backbone, 5 seeds\n  - 4.2 Expert Specialization (Table 1: cross-eval matrix, Table 2: specialization delta +12-20%)\n  - 4.3 Ablation Ladder (Table 3: Frozen 53.6 → Universal 64.8 → Meta-Brain 78.4 → Oracle 79.6)\n  - 4.4 Oracle Routing (Table 5: PAR-Oracle gap 1.2%)\n  - 4.5 FTM Comparison (Table 6: FTM 68.3% overall)\n  - 4.6 Parameter Efficiency (Table 7: VLA-GSE on different backbone Qwen3-VL-4B)\n  - 4.7 CM Ablation (Table 8: +2.4%)\n  - 4.8 Router Architecture (Table 9: Linear/MLP/Attention)\n  - 4.9 6-class vs 3-class (Table 10)\n  - 4.10 Cross-backbone OpenVLA (Table 11)\n  - 4.11 Layer Placement (Tables 12-13)\n- Section 5: Discussion\n- Section 6: Conclusion\n- References: 8 entries [1]-[8]\n\n## Related work cited\n1. LoRA [Hu et al., ICLR 2022]\n2. LIBERO-Plus [Liu et al., CVPR 2026]\n3. VLA-GSE [Wang et al., CoRL 2026]\n4. SAFE [Chen et al., arXiv 2025]\n5. FTM/FLA [Li et al., ICRA 2025]\n6. RobustVLA [Zhang et al., RSS 2026]\n7. pi0 [Physical Intelligence, arXiv 2024]\n8. OpenVLA [Kim et al., CoRL 2024]\n\nAlso mentioned but not cited: MoIRA, CORAL, LoRA-SP\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is the core contribution? Is \"perturbation-diagnostic routing\" a genuinely new idea or a straightforward combination of known techniques (probing + MoE routing + LoRA)? How does it compare to the rich MoE/routing literature?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are there important missing references? The paper mentions MoIRA, CORAL, LoRA-SP without citations. Are there other relevant works in:\n   - Mixture of LoRA experts (MoLoRA, LoRAMoE, etc.)\n   - Failure detection/diagnosis in robotics\n   - Domain-specific adaptation / domain routing\n   - Test-time adaptation for robustness\n   - Robust visual policy transfer beyond FTM\n3. **Positioning and differentiation**: Does the paper adequately distinguish itself from SAFE (which also probes VLA internals for perturbation detection) and VLA-GSE (which also uses MoE routing)?\n4. **Contribution significance**: Are the three claimed contributions truly distinct, or is this one contribution (the system) presented as three?\n5. **Broader impact and limitations**: Are limitations honestly discussed? Is the simulation-only evaluation adequate for CoRL?\n6. **Writing quality and clarity**: Is the paper well-written? Is the narrative coherent?\n\nStructure your review as:\n### Summary\n### Strengths (S1, S2, ...)\n### Weaknesses (W1, W2, ...)\n### Questions for Authors (Q1, Q2, ...)\n### Missing References\n### Minor Issues\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Identify both genuine weaknesses and genuine strengths."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TS7ZtYzArmC2BQVRofu5TC","session_id":"1f5e0932-32f2-4b4b-9817-0d2ade2fda2a","uuid":"8ca42239-a399-44bb-80c7-84d93ad067e6","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:28:55.757Z"}
