{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EMNWQGdRLmMnJLVgzsT7iu","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[conference]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n7\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n8\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n9\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n10\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n11\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n12\t\\usepackage{caption}\n13\t\n14\t\\newcommand{\\ours}{Meta-Brain}\n15\t\\newcommand{\\liberoplus}{LIBERO-Plus}\n16\t\n17\t\\title{Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic Routing of LoRA Experts\\\\for Parameter-Efficient VLA Robustness}\n18\t\n19\t\\author{\n20\t  Anonymous Authors\\\\\n21\t  Submitted to CoRL 2026\n22\t}\n23\t\n24\t\\date{}\n25\t\n26\t\\begin{document}\n27\t\\maketitle\n28\t\n29\t% ============================================================\n30\t\\begin{abstract}\n31\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) achieve over 90\\% success on standard manipulation benchmarks but collapse under environmental perturbations---frozen pi0 drops to 53.6\\% on \\liberoplus{}.\n32\tExisting robustness methods either apply perturbation-agnostic uniform adaptation or require prohibitive parameter budgets (e.g., VLA-GSE: 114M trainable parameters).\n33\tWe observe that VLA internal representations already encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy: a linear probe on PaliGemma layer~6 activations achieves 3-class macro-F1$=$0.993 in a single forward pass.\n34\tBuilding on this finding, we propose \\ours{}, a modular framework that \\emph{diagnoses} the perturbation type from frozen activations via a lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR, 48K parameters), then \\emph{routes} to one of three type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual, Spatial, Physical) for targeted correction.\n35\tOn \\liberoplus{}, \\ours{} achieves 78.4\\% with 6.1M trainable parameters, improving 24.8\\% over the frozen baseline and 13.6\\% over a parameter-matched universal LoRA ($p<0.001$).\n36\tWith an optional Correction Memory module, performance reaches 80.8\\%, closing 90\\% of the gap to VLA-GSE (81.2\\%, 114M params) at 19$\\times$ fewer trainable parameters.\n37\tCross-backbone validation on OpenVLA confirms the generalizability of perturbation-diagnostic routing (gain over universal LoRA: +11.8\\%).\n38\t\\end{abstract}\n39\t\n40\t% ============================================================\n41\t\\section{Introduction}\n42\t\n43\tVision-Language-Action models (VLAs) have demonstrated strong performance on standard manipulation benchmarks, yet they remain brittle when deployed in environments that differ from their training distribution.\n44\tOn \\liberoplus{}---a robustness benchmark that introduces six types of environmental perturbations---frozen pi0 drops from $>$90\\% to 53.6\\% success rate.\n45\t\n46\tDifferent perturbation types disrupt different processing stages within the VLA.\n47\tVisual perturbations (camera angle, lighting, background clutter, sensor noise) corrupt early visual representations.\n48\tSpatial perturbations (object layout changes) invalidate mid-level spatial reasoning.\n49\tPhysical perturbations (robot dynamics changes) break late-stage action planning.\n50\tYet existing robustness methods treat all perturbations uniformly: VLA-GSE~\\cite{vla-gse} routes by per-token capacity needs without perturbation awareness, requiring 114M trainable parameters; FTM~\\cite{ftm} effectively corrects visual perturbations but cannot address spatial or physical failures; RobustVLA~\\cite{robustvla} applies uniform adversarial training across all perturbation types.\n51\t\n52\tWe make three observations that motivate a new approach.\n53\tFirst, VLA internal activations already encode perturbation type: a linear probe on a single layer of frozen pi0 (PaliGemma layer~6) classifies perturbation type with F1$=$0.993 (\\textbf{Claim~1}: perturbation diagnosability).\n54\tSecond, type-specialized LoRA experts dramatically outperform a universal LoRA of equivalent total capacity: routed correction yields +13.6\\% over uniform correction ($p<0.001$), indicating that the right fix for the right failure is fundamentally more efficient than a one-size-fits-all approach (\\textbf{Claim~2}: targeted $>$ uniform correction).\n55\tThird, by leveraging this diagnostic signal for lightweight routing, \\ours{} achieves competitive robustness with VLA-GSE at 19$\\times$ fewer parameters (\\textbf{Claim~3}: parameter-efficient robustness).\n56\t\n57\tOur contributions are:\n58\t\\begin{enumerate}\n59\t  \\item An empirical finding that VLA internal representations encode perturbation type at single-layer, single-step granularity, enabling real-time diagnosis without external modules.\n60\t  \\item \\ours{}: a modular framework comprising a Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR) and an Adaptive LoRA Bank (ALB) that routes corrective experts by diagnosed perturbation type, with an optional Correction Memory (CM) module.\n61\t  \\item A systematic design-dimension analysis comparing perturbation-diagnostic routing against capacity-based routing and universal adaptation, establishing perturbation-awareness as an orthogonal and composable design axis.\n62\t\\end{enumerate}\n63\t\n64\t% ============================================================\n65\t\\section{Related Work}\n66\t\n67\t\\paragraph{VLA Robustness.}\n68\t\\liberoplus{}~\\cite{libero-plus} introduced a systematic perturbation benchmark for VLAs covering visual, spatial, and physical perturbation types.\n69\tSAFE~\\cite{safe} demonstrated that VLA internal features can detect distribution shifts but did not close the loop with correction.\n70\tVLA-GSE~\\cite{vla-gse} achieves 81.2\\% on \\liberoplus{} via capacity-based MoE routing with 114M trainable parameters on Qwen3-VL-4B.\n71\tRobustVLA~\\cite{robustvla} applies adversarial training for +12.6\\% uniform robustness gain.\n72\tNone of these methods combine perturbation diagnosis with type-specific correction.\n73\t\n74\t\\paragraph{Parameter-Efficient Adaptation.}\n75\tLoRA~\\cite{lora} and its variants provide parameter-efficient fine-tuning by injecting low-rank updates into frozen model layers.\n76\tRecent work has explored task-specific routing of LoRA modules: MoIRA and CORAL route by task identity, while LoRA-SP routes by skill category.\n77\tFTM/FLA~\\cite{ftm} modulates visual features with extremely few parameters (4K) but is limited to visual perturbations.\n78\tOur work introduces a new routing dimension---perturbation type---that is orthogonal to task or capacity routing.\n79\t\n80\t% ============================================================\n81\t\\section{Method}\n82\t\n83\t\\ours{} operates in two phases on top of a frozen VLA backbone (pi0 with PaliGemma vision encoder).\n84\t\n85\t\\subsection{Phase 0: Perturbation Diagnosis}\n86\t\n87\tWe extract activations from a single intermediate layer of the frozen vision encoder (PaliGemma layer~6) during the standard forward pass.\n88\tA linear probe trained on these activations classifies the current observation into one of three perturbation categories: \\textbf{Visual} (camera, lighting, background, noise), \\textbf{Spatial} (layout), or \\textbf{Physical} (robot dynamics).\n89\t\n90\tThis classification achieves 3-class macro-F1$=$0.993 on held-out data, confirming that the perturbation-type signal is already present in the frozen activations and does not require any additional forward passes or external detectors.\n91\tThe mechanistic basis aligns with the VLA's layered information processing: visual perturbations primarily disrupt early layers, spatial perturbations affect mid-level representations, and physical perturbations impact late-stage action planning.\n92\t\n93\t\\subsection{Phase 1: Meta-Brain Architecture}\n94\t\n95\t\\paragraph{Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR).}\n96\tThe PAR is a 2-layer MLP (48K parameters) that takes the mean-pooled layer-6 activations and outputs a 3-dimensional soft routing weight vector $\\mathbf{w} \\in \\Delta^2$ via softmax.\n97\tAt inference time, these weights determine the contribution of each LoRA expert.\n98\t\n99\t\\paragraph{Adaptive LoRA Bank (ALB).}\n100\tThe ALB consists of three frozen, pre-trained rank-16 LoRA experts:\n101\t\\begin{itemize}\n102\t  \\item \\textbf{LoRA-V} (Visual): trained on visual perturbation data, applied to PaliGemma layers.\n103\t  \\item \\textbf{LoRA-S} (Spatial): trained on spatial perturbation data.\n104\t  \\item \\textbf{LoRA-P} (Physical): trained on physical perturbation data.\n105\t\\end{itemize}\n106\tEach expert contributes $\\sim$2M parameters; the total system has $\\sim$6.1M trainable parameters.\n107\tThe corrected output is computed as:\n108\t\\begin{equation}\n109\t  h' = h + \\sum_{k \\in \\{V,S,P\\}} w_k \\cdot \\text{LoRA}_k(h)\n110\t\\end{equation}\n111\twhere $h$ is the frozen activation and $w_k$ is the routing weight for expert $k$.\n112\t\n113\t\\paragraph{Correction Memory (CM, optional).}\n114\tCM maintains a FAISS index of exemplar activations from successful rollouts. At inference time, it retrieves $k$-nearest neighbors and modulates the routing weights with a learned blending coefficient $\\alpha$:\n115\t\\begin{equation}\n116\t  \\mathbf{w}' = (1 - \\alpha) \\cdot \\mathbf{w}_{\\text{PAR}} + \\alpha \\cdot \\mathbf{w}_{\\text{CM}}\n117\t\\end{equation}\n118\tThe learned $\\alpha=0.18$ ensures CM provides a gentle correction without overriding the PAR signal.\n119\tCM adds only 1 learnable parameter, a 12MB FAISS index, and 0.8ms retrieval latency.\n120\t\n121\t\\paragraph{End-to-End Training.}\n122\tThe three LoRA experts are first trained independently on type-specific data, then frozen.\n123\tOnly the PAR routing weights are fine-tuned end-to-end on \\liberoplus{} rollouts, optimizing task success as the objective.\n124\t\n125\t% ============================================================\n126\t\\section{Experiments}\n127\t\n128\t\\subsection{Setup}\n129\t\n130\tWe evaluate on \\liberoplus{} with six perturbation types across three categories: Visual (camera, light, background, noise), Spatial (layout), and Physical (robot).\n131\tAll experiments use pi0 with frozen PaliGemma as the base VLA unless otherwise noted.\n132\tResults are averaged over 5 seeds; we report mean $\\pm$ std and conduct paired $t$-tests for significance.\n133\t\n134\t\\subsection{Expert Specialization Analysis}\n135\t\\label{sec:expert_spec}\n136\t\n137\tWe first validate that type-specialized LoRA experts learn perturbation-specific corrections rather than general robustness.\n138\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross_eval} shows the cross-evaluation matrix: each expert is evaluated on all perturbation types.\n139\t\n140\t\\begin{table}[h]\n141\t\\centering\n142\t\\caption{Expert cross-evaluation matrix (success rate \\%). Each expert is trained on one perturbation category and evaluated across all six types.}\n143\t\\label{tab:cross_eval}\n144\t\\small\n145\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc|c}\n146\t\\toprule\n147\tExpert & cam. & light & bg. & noise & layout & robot & Overall \\\\\n148\t\\midrule\n149\tLoRA-V & 74.0 & 80.0 & 78.0 & 72.0 & 60.0 & 54.0 & 69.7 \\\\\n150\tLoRA-S & 44.0 & 52.0 & 48.0 & 42.0 & \\textbf{90.0} & 56.0 & 55.3 \\\\\n151\tLoRA-P & 40.0 & 50.0 & 46.0 & 40.0 & 62.0 & \\textbf{88.0} & 54.3 \\\\\n152\tUniversal & 54.0 & 66.0 & 62.0 & 54.0 & 78.0 & 74.0 & 64.8 \\\\\n153\tFrozen pi0 & 42.0 & 58.0 & 52.0 & 46.0 & 68.0 & 56.0 & 53.6 \\\\\n154\t\\bottomrule\n155\t\\end{tabular}\n156\t\\end{table}\n157\t\n158\tEach specialist dominates on its target category but degrades severely on non-target types (cross-type degradation up to $-$20\\% vs.\\ frozen baseline).\n159\tThis asymmetry confirms that the three perturbation categories require fundamentally incompatible correction patterns, and that routing---not simply adding parameters---is necessary.\n160\t\n161\tTable~\\ref{tab:spec_delta} quantifies the specialization advantage: own-type experts outperform the universal LoRA by +12\\% to +20\\%, with camera type showing the largest gap.\n162\t\n163\t\\begin{table}[h]\n164\t\\centering\n165\t\\caption{Specialization delta: own-type expert vs.\\ universal LoRA.}\n166\t\\label{tab:spec_delta}\n167\t\\small\n168\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n169\t\\toprule\n170\tPerturbation & Specialist & Universal & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n171\t\\midrule\n172\tcamera & 74.0 & 54.0 & +20.0 \\\\\n173\tlight & 80.0 & 66.0 & +14.0 \\\\\n174\tbackground & 78.0 & 62.0 & +16.0 \\\\\n175\tnoise & 72.0 & 54.0 & +18.0 \\\\\n176\tlayout & 90.0 & 78.0 & +12.0 \\\\\n177\trobot & 88.0 & 74.0 & +14.0 \\\\\n178\t\\bottomrule\n179\t\\end{tabular}\n180\t\\end{table}\n181\t\n182\t\\subsection{Main Result: Ablation Ladder}\n183\t\\label{sec:ablation_ladder}\n184\t\n185\tTable~\\ref{tab:ablation_ladder} presents the systematic ablation from frozen baseline to oracle routing, providing the core evidence for Claim~2.\n186\t\n187\t\\begin{table}[h]\n188\t\\centering\n189\t\\caption{Ablation ladder on \\liberoplus{} (5 seeds). \\ours{} gains +13.6\\% over universal LoRA ($p<0.001$); the gap to oracle routing is only 1.2\\% ($p=0.14$, n.s.).}\n190\t\\label{tab:ablation_ladder}\n191\t\\small\n192\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n193\t\\toprule\n194\tMethod & Params & Overall $\\pm$ std & \\makecell{Gain over\\\\Frozen} & \\makecell{Gain over\\\\Prev.\\ Step} \\\\\n195\t\\midrule\n196\tFrozen pi0 & 0 & 53.6 $\\pm$ 1.4 & --- & --- \\\\\n197\tUniversal LoRA (r16) & $\\sim$2M & 64.8 $\\pm$ 1.8 & +11.2 & +11.2 \\\\\n198\t\\ours{} (PAR+ALB) & $\\sim$6M & 78.4 $\\pm$ 1.6 & +24.8 & +13.6 \\\\\n199\tOracle routing & $\\sim$6M & 79.6 $\\pm$ 1.2 & +26.0 & +1.2 \\\\\n200\t\\bottomrule\n201\t\\end{tabular}\n202\t\\end{table}\n203\t\n204\tThe ablation reveals a clear three-step ladder.\n205\tUniversal LoRA provides +11.2\\% baseline robustness over the frozen model, demonstrating that LoRA adaptation on PaliGemma layers is an effective correction mechanism.\n206\t\\ours{}'s perturbation-diagnostic routing adds a further +13.6\\% ($p<0.001$), exceeding the gain from LoRA adaptation itself---routing is not marginal optimization but a primary contributor.\n207\tThe oracle-PAR gap of only 1.2\\% ($p=0.14$, not significant) confirms that the PAR router has nearly saturated the routing upper bound, validating that Phase~0's diagnostic signal is actionable for downstream correction.\n208\t\n209\tTable~\\ref{tab:per_perturb} provides the per-perturbation breakdown across all methods.\n210\t\n211\t\\begin{table}[h]\n212\t\\centering\n213\t\\caption{\\ours{} per-perturbation results on \\liberoplus{}.}\n214\t\\label{tab:per_perturb}\n215\t\\small\n216\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n217\t\\toprule\n218\tPerturbation & Frozen & Universal & \\ours{} & Oracle & \\makecell{Gain vs\\\\Frozen} & \\makecell{Gain vs\\\\Univ.} \\\\\n219\t\\midrule\n220\tcamera & 42.0 & 54.0 & 72.0 & 74.0 & +30.0 & +18.0 \\\\\n221\tlight & 58.0 & 66.0 & 78.0 & 78.0 & +20.0 & +12.0 \\\\\n222\tbackground & 52.0 & 62.0 & 76.0 & 78.0 & +24.0 & +14.0 \\\\\n223\tnoise & 46.0 & 54.0 & 70.0 & 72.0 & +24.0 & +16.0 \\\\\n224\tlayout & 68.0 & 78.0 & 88.0 & 88.0 & +20.0 & +10.0 \\\\\n225\trobot & 56.0 & 74.0 & 86.0 & 88.0 & +30.0 & +12.0 \\\\\n226\t\\midrule\n227\t\\textbf{Overall} & 53.6 & 64.8 & \\textbf{78.4} & 79.6 & +24.8 & +13.6 \\\\\n228\t\\bottomrule\n229\t\\end{tabular}\n230\t\\end{table}\n231\t\n232\t\\ours{} improves on all six perturbation types, with the largest gains on camera (+30.0) and robot (+30.0), which are the types where frozen pi0 is weakest.\n233\tThe PAR routing achieves a diagonality score of 0.92, meaning 92\\% of routing weight is correctly assigned to the target expert.\n234\t\n235\t\\subsection{Oracle Routing Ablation}\n236\t\\label{sec:oracle}\n237\t\n238\tTo isolate routing quality from expert quality, we replace the PAR router with ground-truth perturbation labels (hard oracle routing).\n239\tTable~\\ref{tab:oracle} shows the per-perturbation gap.\n240\t\n241\t\\begin{table}[h]\n242\t\\centering\n243\t\\caption{Oracle vs.\\ PAR routing gap. The overall gap of 1.2\\% confirms PAR nearly saturates the routing upper bound.}\n244\t\\label{tab:oracle}\n245\t\\small\n246\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n247\t\\toprule\n248\tPerturbation & PAR & Oracle & Gap \\\\\n249\t\\midrule\n250\tcamera & 72.0 & 74.0 & 2.0 \\\\\n251\tlight & 78.0 & 78.0 & 0.0 \\\\\n252\tbackground & 76.0 & 78.0 & 2.0 \\\\\n253\tnoise & 70.0 & 72.0 & 2.0 \\\\\n254\tlayout & 88.0 & 88.0 & 0.0 \\\\\n255\trobot & 86.0 & 88.0 & 2.0 \\\\\n256\t\\midrule\n257\t\\textbf{Overall} & 78.4 & 79.6 & 1.2 \\\\\n258\t\\bottomrule\n259\t\\end{tabular}\n260\t\\end{table}\n261\t\n262\tThe gap concentrates on visual subtypes (camera, background, noise: 2.0\\% each) where intra-category confusion is highest. Layout and light show zero gap, as these are the most separable categories in the Phase~0 feature space.\n263\t\n264\t\\subsection{Comparison with FTM}\n265\t\\label{sec:ftm}\n266\t\n267\tFTM represents a strong visual-only baseline that modulates features with only 4K parameters.\n268\tTable~\\ref{tab:ftm} compares FTM with \\ours{} across all perturbation types.\n269\t\n270\t\\begin{table}[h]\n271\t\\centering\n272\t\\caption{FTM vs.\\ \\ours{} comparison. FTM is competitive on visual perturbations but cannot address spatial/physical types.}\n273\t\\label{tab:ftm}\n274\t\\small\n275\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n276\t\\toprule\n277\tPerturbation & FTM (4K) & \\ours{} (6M) & LoRA-V (2M) & Univ.\\ (2M) \\\\\n278\t\\midrule\n279\tcamera & 70.0 & 72.0 & 74.0 & 54.0 \\\\\n280\tlight & 76.0 & 78.0 & 80.0 & 66.0 \\\\\n281\tbackground & 72.0 & 76.0 & 78.0 & 62.0 \\\\\n282\tnoise & 68.0 & 70.0 & 72.0 & 54.0 \\\\\n283\tlayout & 68.0 & \\textbf{88.0} & 60.0 & 78.0 \\\\\n284\trobot & 56.0 & \\textbf{86.0} & 54.0 & 74.0 \\\\\n285\t\\midrule\n286\tOverall & 68.3 & \\textbf{78.4} & 69.7 & 64.8 \\\\\n287\t\\bottomrule\n288\t\\end{tabular}\n289\t\\end{table}\n290\t\n291\tFTM achieves 71.5\\% average on visual subtypes (close to LoRA-V's 76.0\\%), confirming its effectiveness for visual correction.\n292\tHowever, FTM's design assumption---that perturbations are confined to the visual channel---causes it to achieve only 68.0\\% on layout and 56.0\\% on robot, comparable to or below the frozen baseline.\n293\t\\ours{} outperforms FTM by +10.1\\% overall, with the decisive advantage on non-visual types (+22.0\\% average on layout+robot).\n294\t\n295\t\\subsection{Parameter Efficiency Analysis}\n296\t\\label{sec:param_eff}\n297\t\n298\tTable~\\ref{tab:param_eff} compares parameter efficiency across methods, providing core evidence for Claim~3.\n299\t\n300\t\\begin{table}[h]\n301\t\\centering\n302\t\\caption{Parameter efficiency comparison. Gain/M-param measures robustness gain per million trainable parameters. $^*$VLA-GSE uses Qwen3-VL-4B backbone; gain is over its own frozen baseline.}\n303\t\\label{tab:param_eff}\n304\t\\small\n305\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n306\t\\toprule\n307\tMethod & Backbone & \\makecell{Trainable\\\\Params} & \\makecell{Gain over\\\\Frozen} & Gain/M \\\\\n308\t\\midrule\n309\tFTM & pi0 & 4K & 14.7 & 3675.0 \\\\\n310\tUniversal LoRA (r16) & pi0 & 2.0M & 11.2 & 5.6 \\\\\n311\t\\ours{} & pi0 & 6.1M & 24.8 & 4.07 \\\\\n312\t\\ours{} + CM & pi0 & 6.1M & 27.2 & 4.46 \\\\\n313\tVLA-GSE$^*$ & Qwen3-VL-4B & 114M & 27.6 & 0.24 \\\\\n314\t\\bottomrule\n315\t\\end{tabular}\n316\t\\end{table}\n317\t\n318\t\\ours{} achieves 90\\% of VLA-GSE's gain (24.8 vs.\\ 27.6) with only 5.3\\% of the trainable parameters, yielding a 17$\\times$ advantage in gain per million parameters (4.07 vs.\\ 0.24).\n319\tWith Correction Memory, \\ours{}+CM reaches 80.8\\% (gain of 27.2), further closing the gap to VLA-GSE's 81.2\\% while maintaining the parameter advantage.\n320\tFTM achieves extraordinary per-parameter efficiency on visual perturbations (3675 gain/M) but its scope is limited to a single perturbation category.\n321\t\\ours{} occupies the Pareto-optimal position in the gain--parameter trade-off space: substantially more efficient than VLA-GSE, with multi-type coverage that FTM lacks.\n322\t\n323\t\\subsection{Correction Memory Ablation}\n324\t\\label{sec:cm}\n325\t\n326\tTable~\\ref{tab:cm} presents the ablation of the optional Correction Memory module.\n327\t\n328\t\\begin{table}[h]\n329\t\\centering\n330\t\\caption{Correction Memory ablation. CM provides +2.4\\% gain with 1 learned parameter and 0.8ms latency.}\n331\t\\label{tab:cm}\n332\t\\small\n333\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n334\t\\toprule\n335\tPerturbation & w/o CM & w/ CM & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n336\t\\midrule\n337\tcamera & 72.0 & 74.0 & +2.0 \\\\\n338\tlight & 78.0 & 80.0 & +2.0 \\\\\n339\tbackground & 76.0 & 78.0 & +2.0 \\\\\n340\tnoise & 70.0 & 74.0 & +4.0 \\\\\n341\tlayout & 88.0 & 90.0 & +2.0 \\\\\n342\trobot & 86.0 & 89.0 & +3.0 \\\\\n343\t\\midrule\n344\tOverall & 78.4 & \\textbf{80.8} & +2.4 \\\\\n345\t\\bottomrule\n346\t\\end{tabular}\n347\t\\end{table}\n348\t\n349\tCM provides a consistent +2.0--4.0\\% improvement across all perturbation types, with the largest gain on noise (+4.0\\%).\n350\tThe noise category benefits most because sensor noise and camera perturbations occupy nearby regions in activation space, causing the highest PAR routing uncertainty; CM's exemplar matching disambiguates these borderline cases.\n351\tThe learned blending coefficient $\\alpha=0.18$ confirms that CM applies gentle corrections without overriding the PAR signal.\n352\tWith only 1 additional learnable parameter, a 12MB FAISS index, and 0.8ms retrieval latency, CM is a low-cost plug-in module.\n353\t\n354\t\\subsection{Router Architecture Comparison}\n355\t\\label{sec:router_arch}\n356\t\n357\tWe compare three PAR router architectures to validate our choice of a 2-layer MLP.\n358\t\n359\t\\begin{table}[h]\n360\t\\centering\n361\t\\caption{Router architecture comparison on \\liberoplus{}.}\n362\t\\label{tab:router}\n363\t\\small\n364\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n365\t\\toprule\n366\tRouter & Params & Routing Acc (\\%) & Downstream (\\%) & $\\Delta$ vs MLP \\\\\n367\t\\midrule\n368\tLinear & 6K & 94.1 & 76.6 & $-$1.8 \\\\\n369\t2-layer MLP & 48K & 97.2 & 78.4 & --- \\\\\n370\tAttention-based & 196K & 97.8 & 78.8 & +0.4 \\\\\n371\t\\bottomrule\n372\t\\end{tabular}\n373\t\\end{table}\n374\t\n375\tA linear router already achieves 94.1\\% routing accuracy (downstream 76.6\\%), confirming that the Phase~0 classification boundary is nearly linear.\n376\tThe 2-layer MLP captures residual nonlinearities for a +1.8\\% downstream improvement.\n377\tAn attention-based router offers negligible further gain (+0.4\\%) at 4$\\times$ the parameters, confirming that a lightweight MLP is sufficient.\n378\t\n379\t\\subsection{Routing Granularity: 6-Class vs.\\ 3-Class}\n380\t\\label{sec:granularity}\n381\t\n382\tWe test whether finer-grained 6-class routing (one expert per perturbation subtype) improves over 3-class routing.\n383\t\n384\t\\begin{table}[h]\n385\t\\centering\n386\t\\caption{6-class vs.\\ 3-class routing. Finer granularity yields only +1.0\\% at 2$\\times$ the parameter cost.}\n387\t\\label{tab:granularity}\n388\t\\small\n389\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc|c}\n390\t\\toprule\n391\tConfig & cam. & light & bg. & noise & layout & robot & Overall \\\\\n392\t\\midrule\n393\t3-class & 72.0 & 78.0 & 76.0 & 70.0 & \\textbf{88.0} & \\textbf{86.0} & 78.4 \\\\\n394\t6-class & 74.0 & 80.0 & 78.0 & 74.0 & 88.0 & 82.0 & 79.4 \\\\\n395\t$\\Delta$ & +2.0 & +2.0 & +2.0 & +4.0 & 0.0 & $-$4.0 & +1.0 \\\\\n396\t\\bottomrule\n397\t\\end{tabular}\n398\t\\end{table}\n399\t\n400\tThe 6-class configuration provides modest gains on visual subtypes (+1.5\\% average) where finer discrimination is beneficial, but shows a $-$4.0\\% regression on robot---the increased softmax denominator dilutes routing confidence for single-subtype categories.\n401\tAt 12.2M parameters (2$\\times$ the 3-class budget), the marginal gain of +1.0\\% does not justify the additional complexity, validating 3-class routing as the efficiency sweet spot.\n402\t\n403\t\\subsection{Cross-Backbone Validation}\n404\t\\label{sec:cross_backbone}\n405\t\n406\tTo test generalizability beyond pi0, we replicate the full \\ours{} pipeline on OpenVLA (SigLIP + Llama-2-7B).\n407\t\n408\t\\begin{table}[h]\n409\t\\centering\n410\t\\caption{Cross-backbone validation. Perturbation-diagnostic routing generalizes to OpenVLA with consistent gain patterns.}\n411\t\\label{tab:cross_backbone}\n412\t\\small\n413\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n414\t\\toprule\n415\tMetric & pi0 (PaliGemma) & OpenVLA (SigLIP) \\\\\n416\t\\midrule\n417\tPhase 0 3-class F1 & 0.993 & 0.972 \\\\\n418\tBest probe layer & 6 & 8 \\\\\n419\tFrozen baseline (\\%) & 53.6 & 48.2 \\\\\n420\tUniversal LoRA (\\%) & 64.8 & 58.6 \\\\\n421\t\\ours{} (\\%) & 78.4 & 70.4 \\\\\n422\tOracle (\\%) & 79.6 & 71.8 \\\\\n423\tGain over frozen & +24.8 & +22.2 \\\\\n424\tGain over universal & +13.6 & +11.8 \\\\\n425\tOracle--PAR gap & 1.2 & 1.4 \\\\\n426\tRouting diagonality & 0.92 & 0.89 \\\\\n427\t\\bottomrule\n428\t\\end{tabular}\n429\t\\end{table}\n430\t\n431\tDespite architectural differences (SigLIP vs.\\ PaliGemma, Llama-2-7B vs.\\ Gemma action head), the gain patterns are highly consistent:\n432\tPhase~0 achieves F1$=$0.972 at layer~8 (shifted from layer~6 due to different encoder depth);\n433\tthe gain over universal LoRA is +11.8\\% (vs.\\ +13.6\\% on pi0);\n434\tand the oracle--PAR gap remains small at 1.4\\%.\n435\tThe lower absolute performance on OpenVLA (70.4\\% vs.\\ 78.4\\%) reflects its weaker frozen baseline (48.2\\% vs.\\ 53.6\\%), not a failure of the routing mechanism.\n436\tThese results confirm that perturbation diagnosability is a general property of VLA representations, not an artifact of a specific architecture.\n437\t\n438\t\\subsection{LoRA Layer Placement Ablation}\n439\t\\label{sec:layer_placement}\n440\t\n441\tWe investigate whether optimal LoRA layer placement differs by perturbation type, testing three layer ranges: early (2--8), mid (6--12), and late (12--18).\n442\t\n443\t\\begin{table}[h]\n444\t\\centering\n445\t\\caption{Layer placement $\\times$ expert type (own-type success rate \\%). Optimal placement aligns with the VLA's layered processing hierarchy.}\n446\t\\label{tab:layer_place}\n447\t\\small\n448\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n449\t\\toprule\n450\tExpert & Early (2--8) & Mid (6--12) & Late (12--18) & All layers \\\\\n451\t\\midrule\n452\tLoRA-V (Visual) & \\textbf{77.2} & 72.8 & 68.4 & 76.0 \\\\\n453\tLoRA-S (Spatial) & 82.0 & \\textbf{91.4} & 86.0 & 90.0 \\\\\n454\tLoRA-P (Physical) & 78.0 & 82.0 & \\textbf{89.2} & 88.0 \\\\\n455\t\\bottomrule\n456\t\\end{tabular}\n457\t\\end{table}\n458\t\n459\tThe optimal layer placement forms a clear diagonal: LoRA-V peaks at early layers (77.2\\%), LoRA-S at mid layers (91.4\\%), and LoRA-P at late layers (89.2\\%).\n460\tThis aligns with the mechanistic hypothesis: visual perturbations corrupt early visual encoding, spatial perturbations disrupt mid-level spatial reasoning, and physical perturbations affect late-stage action planning.\n461\t\n462\t\\begin{table}[h]\n463\t\\centering\n464\t\\caption{Type-aligned layer placement achieves +1.8\\% over uniform all-layers placement while using 60\\% fewer parameters per expert.}\n465\t\\label{tab:layer_system}\n466\t\\small\n467\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n468\t\\toprule\n469\tConfiguration & Params/expert & Visual & Spatial & Physical & Overall \\\\\n470\t\\midrule\n471\tAll layers (default) & $\\sim$2.0M & 76.0 & 90.0 & 88.0 & 78.4 \\\\\n472\tType-aligned layers & $\\sim$0.8M & 77.2 & 91.4 & 89.2 & 80.2 \\\\\n473\t\\bottomrule\n474\t\\end{tabular}\n475\t\\end{table}\n476\t\n477\tType-aligned placement improves overall performance by +1.8\\% while reducing per-expert parameters by 60\\%.\n478\tThis provides further mechanistic evidence for the perturbation-type-specific nature of VLA internal processing and opens a direction for even more parameter-efficient configurations.\n479\t\n480\t% ============================================================\n481\t\\section{Discussion}\n482\t\n483\t\\paragraph{Why routing matters more than capacity.}\n484\tThe ablation ladder (Table~\\ref{tab:ablation_ladder}) shows that routing contributes 13.6\\% of the total 26.0\\% gain (52\\%), rivaling the contribution of LoRA adaptation itself (11.2\\%, 43\\%).\n485\tThe remaining 1.2\\% (5\\%) is the routing precision headroom.\n486\tThis decomposition establishes perturbation-diagnostic routing as a primary---not auxiliary---design dimension for VLA robustness.\n487\t\n488\t\\paragraph{Easy diagnosis $\\neq$ trivial contribution.}\n489\tThe near-perfect Phase~0 classification (F1$=$0.993) might suggest routing is trivially solvable and therefore uninteresting.\n490\tWe argue the opposite: easy routing is \\emph{desirable} because it means the diagnostic signal adds zero overhead and zero risk of routing error.\n491\tThe value lies in what happens \\emph{after} routing: specialized correction yields +13.6\\% over uniform correction.\n492\t\n493\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n494\t(1) Our evaluation is simulation-only (\\liberoplus{}). While this follows the established pattern for VLA architecture contributions at CoRL (SAFE, VLA-GSE), real-robot validation remains future work.\n495\t(2) The 3-class taxonomy is validated for \\liberoplus{}'s perturbation set; novel perturbation categories would require extending the expert bank.\n496\t(3) Cross-backbone comparison with VLA-GSE is gain-over-baseline rather than absolute, since the two systems use different backbones.\n497\t\n498\t% ============================================================\n499\t\\section{Conclusion}\n500\t\n501\tWe presented \\ours{}, a framework that leverages the perturbation-type information already encoded in VLA internal representations to route type-specialized LoRA experts for targeted robustness correction.\n502\tThree findings support our approach:\n503\t(1) VLA activations at a single layer classify perturbation type with F1$=$0.993, enabling zero-overhead real-time diagnosis;\n504\t(2) routed type-specific correction outperforms uniform correction by 13.6\\% ($p<0.001$), demonstrating that targeted correction is fundamentally more capacity-efficient than uniform adaptation;\n505\t(3) \\ours{} achieves 78.4\\% (80.8\\% with CM) on \\liberoplus{} with 6.1M parameters, closing 90\\% of the gap to VLA-GSE (81.2\\%, 114M params) at 19$\\times$ fewer trainable parameters.\n506\tCross-backbone validation on OpenVLA confirms the generality of perturbation-diagnostic routing.\n507\t\n508\t\\bibliographystyle{plain}\n509\t\\begin{thebibliography}{10}\n510\t\n511\t\\bibitem{lora}\n512\tE.~J. Hu, Y.~Shen, P.~Wallis, et al.\n513\t\\newblock LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models.\n514\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2022.\n515\t\n516\t\\bibitem{libero-plus}\n517\tB.~Liu, et al.\n518\t\\newblock LIBERO-Plus: A Robustness Benchmark for Vision-Language-Action Models.\n519\t\\newblock In \\emph{CVPR}, 2026.\n520\t\n521\t\\bibitem{vla-gse}\n522\tZ.~Wang, et al.\n523\t\\newblock VLA-GSE: Generalist-Specialist Experts for Robust VLA Deployment.\n524\t\\newblock In \\emph{CoRL}, 2026.\n525\t\n526\t\\bibitem{safe}\n527\tY.~Chen, et al.\n528\t\\newblock SAFE: Sensitivity-Aware Features for Out-of-Distribution Robustness in VLAs.\n529\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n530\t\n531\t\\bibitem{ftm}\n532\tX.~Li, et al.\n533\t\\newblock Feature-Level Modulation for Robust Visual Policy Transfer.\n534\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICRA}, 2025.\n535\t\n536\t\\bibitem{robustvla}\n537\tJ.~Zhang, et al.\n538\t\\newblock RobustVLA: Adversarial Training for Vision-Language-Action Robustness.\n539\t\\newblock In \\emph{RSS}, 2026.\n540\t\n541\t\\bibitem{pi0}\n542\tPhysical Intelligence.\n543\t\\newblock pi0: A Vision-Language-Action Flow Model for General Robot Control.\n544\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2024.\n545\t\n546\t\\bibitem{openvla}\n547\tM.~Kim, et al.\n548\t\\newblock OpenVLA: An Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Model.\n549\t\\newblock In \\emph{CoRL}, 2024.\n550\t\n551\t\\end{thebibliography}\n552\t\n553\t\\end{document}\n554\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TS7ZtYzArmC2BQVRofu5TC","session_id":"1f5e0932-32f2-4b4b-9817-0d2ade2fda2a","uuid":"de662f63-30c1-43a0-a44c-8ce5ac2717c9","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:29:24.684Z"}
