{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TRwXVvJ1GrGoVgBnxdpL8S","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Meta-Brain Experiment Plan\n2\t# Generated: 2026-05-15\n3\t# Claims reference: paper_framing.md\n4\t\n5\texperiments:\n6\t  # ═══════════════ Claim 1: Perturbation Diagnosability ═══════════════\n7\t\n8\t  - id: plan_001\n9\t    claim: 1\n10\t    title: \"Phase 0 — Perturbation classification from frozen activations\"\n11\t    description: >\n12\t      Train 2-layer MLP on frozen pi0 PaliGemma activations for 3-class perturbation\n13\t      classification (Visual/Spatial/Physical). Sweep layers, pooling strategies, learning rates.\n14\t      Includes binary sanity check, 6-class comparison, few-shot K=1/3/5/10 analysis.\n15\t    priority: P0\n16\t    status: done\n17\t    registry_id: phase0_activation_collection\n18\t    gpu_budget_hours: 5\n19\t    deps: []\n20\t    key_results: \"3-class F1=0.993, binary AUC=1.0, K=1 F1=0.898, layer6 K=1=1.0\"\n21\t\n22\t  # ═══════════════ Claim 2: Targeted > Uniform Correction ═══════════════\n23\t\n24\t  - id: plan_002\n25\t    claim: 2\n26\t    title: \"LoRA expert training — 3 perturbation-specific + 1 universal baseline\"\n27\t    description: >\n28\t      Train 4 LoRA adapters on frozen pi0 PaliGemma attention layers (rank 16, Q/K/V/O projections):\n29\t      (a) LoRA-V: trained on Visual perturbation rollouts (camera+light+background+noise)\n30\t      (b) LoRA-S: trained on Spatial perturbation rollouts (layout)\n31\t      (c) LoRA-P: trained on Physical perturbation rollouts (robot)\n32\t      (d) Universal LoRA: trained on mixed all-perturbation rollouts\n33\t      Each on 1 GPU in parallel (4 GPUs total).\n34\t\n35\t      Training data strategy (two-pronged):\n36\t      (a) Visual perturbations (LoRA-V): Use LIBERO standard expert demonstrations with ONLINE\n37\t          visual augmentation during training (camera jitter, brightness/contrast shifts,\n38\t          background texture swap, Gaussian noise). Action labels are unchanged because visual\n39\t          perturbations don't alter the physical task. This is analogous to FTM's training setup.\n40\t      (b) Spatial/Physical perturbations (LoRA-S, LoRA-P): Use LIBERO's scripted oracle policies\n41\t          to collect new expert demonstrations in LIBERO-Plus perturbed environments (layout\n42\t          randomization, robot initial state randomization). ~200 successful demos per category.\n43\t          Fallback: if oracle policy unavailable, use DAgger-style collection with frozen pi0\n44\t          (collect partial successes + human corrections).\n45\t      (c) Universal LoRA: Mix of all above data sources.\n46\t      Training objective: SFT (behavior cloning) — minimize flow-matching action prediction\n47\t      loss on (perturbed_observation, correct_action) pairs. Match pi0's action head format.\n48\t      Convergence criterion: validation action MSE plateaus for 3 consecutive eval checkpoints.\n49\t      LoRA config: rank 16 on Q/K/V/O of ALL PaliGemma attention layers (18 layers).\n50\t      All 3 experts use identical layer coverage initially (per Reviewer feedback — Phase 0\n51\t      shows <2.5pp spread across layers, no evidence for layer-specific assignment).\n52\t      Layer placement ablation deferred to plan_012.\n53\t\n54\t      Evaluate each expert on its own perturbation type AND cross-type (mismatched expert).\n55\t    priority: P0\n56\t    gpu_budget_hours: 55\n57\t    deps: []\n58\t    metrics:\n59\t      - per_expert_success_rate_on_own_type\n60\t      - per_expert_success_rate_cross_type\n61\t      - universal_lora_success_rate_all_types\n62\t\n63\t  - id: plan_003\n64\t    claim: 2\n65\t    title: \"Meta-Brain assembly — PAR router + LoRA bank end-to-end\"\n66\t    description: >\n67\t      Assemble PAR router (PaliGemma layer 6, 2-layer MLP) + 3 LoRA experts with\n68\t      soft routing: h_corrected = h_frozen + sum_k p(type_k) * LoRA_k(h_frozen).\n69\t      End-to-end routing fine-tuning with frozen LoRA parameters (only tune PAR routing weights).\n70\t      Evaluate on full LIBERO-Plus benchmark (all 7 perturbation types × all task suites).\n71\t    priority: P0\n72\t    gpu_budget_hours: 15\n73\t    deps: [plan_002]\n74\t    metrics:\n75\t      - libero_plus_overall_success_rate\n76\t      - per_perturbation_success_rate_breakdown\n77\t      - routing_weight_distribution_per_type\n78\t\n79\t  - id: plan_004\n80\t    claim: 2\n81\t    title: \"Oracle routing ablation — upper bound for routing quality\"\n82\t    description: >\n83\t      Replace PAR router with ground-truth perturbation type labels (oracle routing).\n84\t      Measures the gap between learned routing and perfect routing.\n85\t      If gap is small, PAR is sufficient; if large, router needs improvement.\n86\t    priority: P1\n87\t    gpu_budget_hours: 5\n88\t    deps: [plan_002]\n89\t    metrics:\n90\t      - oracle_vs_par_success_rate_gap\n91\t\n92\t  - id: plan_005\n93\t    claim: 2\n94\t    title: \"Ablation ladder — Frozen → Universal LoRA → Random → Meta-Brain → Oracle\"\n95\t    description: >\n96\t      Systematic comparison on LIBERO-Plus with 5 seeds:\n97\t      (a) Frozen pi0 baseline (no adaptation)\n98\t      (b) Universal LoRA (rank 16, ~2M params)\n99\t      (c) Random routing (randomly assign one of 3 LoRA experts per episode — controls\n100\t          for ensemble effect. If random ≈ Meta-Brain, Claim 2 is falsified)\n101\t      (d) Meta-Brain (PAR + 3 LoRA experts, ~6M params)\n102\t      (e) Oracle routing (ground-truth type → LoRA expert)\n103\t      Report overall success rate + per-perturbation breakdown.\n104\t      This is the core evidence for Claim 2. The critical comparison is (c) vs (d):\n105\t      Meta-Brain must significantly outperform random routing to prove routing matters.\n106\t    priority: P0\n107\t    gpu_budget_hours: 25\n108\t    deps: [plan_003, plan_004]\n109\t    metrics:\n110\t      - ablation_ladder_table\n111\t      - gain_over_frozen_per_method\n112\t      - gain_over_universal_lora\n113\t      - meta_brain_vs_random_routing_gap\n114\t\n115\t  # ═══════════════ Claim 3: Parameter-Efficient Robustness ═══════════════\n116\t\n117\t  - id: plan_006\n118\t    claim: 3\n119\t    title: \"FTM/FLA reproduction — visual-only baseline comparison\"\n120\t    description: >\n121\t      Reproduce FTM on pi0 for visual perturbation types (camera, light, background, noise).\n122\t      Compare Meta-Brain LoRA-V vs FTM on visual perturbations.\n123\t      Meta-Brain must show value beyond FTM by additionally handling spatial/physical.\n124\t    priority: P1\n125\t    gpu_budget_hours: 10\n126\t    deps: []\n127\t    metrics:\n128\t      - ftm_vs_lora_v_on_visual_perturbations\n129\t      - meta_brain_advantage_on_non_visual\n130\t\n131\t  - id: plan_007\n132\t    claim: 3\n133\t    title: \"Parameter efficiency analysis — gain-over-baseline vs trainable params\"\n134\t    description: >\n135\t      Compute gain-over-frozen-baseline per method and normalize by trainable parameters.\n136\t      Plot scatter: x=trainable params (log scale), y=gain-over-baseline.\n137\t      Include: Meta-Brain (12M), Universal LoRA (3.7M), FTM (4K-4.7M), VLA-GSE (114M, from paper).\n138\t      This is the visual evidence for Claim 3.\n139\t    priority: P0\n140\t    gpu_budget_hours: 0\n141\t    deps: [plan_005, plan_006]\n142\t    metrics:\n143\t      - parameter_efficiency_scatter_plot\n144\t      - gain_per_million_params\n145\t\n146\t  # ═══════════════ Tier 2 Ablations ═══════════════\n147\t\n148\t  - id: plan_008\n149\t    claim: 2\n150\t    title: \"Correction Memory ablation — with/without CM\"\n151\t    description: >\n152\t      Compare Meta-Brain with and without Correction Memory module.\n153\t      CM adds routing-weight modulation from retrieved exemplars.\n154\t      Quantify marginal gain of CM on overall and per-perturbation success.\n155\t    priority: P1\n156\t    gpu_budget_hours: 10\n157\t    deps: [plan_003]\n158\t    metrics:\n159\t      - cm_ablation_delta\n160\t      - cm_benefit_per_perturbation_type\n161\t\n162\t  - id: plan_009\n163\t    claim: 1\n164\t    title: \"Router architecture comparison — MLP vs linear vs attention\"\n165\t    description: >\n166\t      Compare PAR variants: (a) 2-layer MLP (default), (b) linear classifier,\n167\t      (c) attention-based router. All on PaliGemma layer 6.\n168\t      Measures whether routing accuracy changes translate to downstream task success.\n169\t    priority: P2\n170\t    gpu_budget_hours: 5\n171\t    deps: [plan_002]\n172\t    metrics:\n173\t      - router_accuracy_comparison\n174\t      - downstream_success_by_router_type\n175\t\n176\t  - id: plan_010\n177\t    claim: 2\n178\t    title: \"6-class vs 3-class routing comparison\"\n179\t    description: >\n180\t      Compare 6-class fine-grained routing (camera/light/background/noise/layout/robot)\n181\t      with 6 LoRA experts vs 3-class coarse routing (Visual/Spatial/Physical) with 3 experts.\n182\t      Tests whether finer-grained routing justifies additional parameters.\n183\t    priority: P2\n184\t    gpu_budget_hours: 15\n185\t    deps: [plan_002]\n186\t    metrics:\n187\t      - 6class_vs_3class_overall_success\n188\t      - marginal_gain_per_additional_expert\n189\t\n190\t  # ═══════════════ Tier 3 Extensions ═══════════════\n191\t\n192\t  - id: plan_011\n193\t    claim: 3\n194\t    title: \"Cross-backbone validation — SmolVLA or OpenVLA\"\n195\t    description: >\n196\t      Replicate Phase 0 + Phase 1 core pipeline on a second VLA backbone\n197\t      (SmolVLA or OpenVLA) to test generalizability of the perturbation-diagnostic\n198\t      routing principle across architectures.\n199\t    priority: P2\n200\t    gpu_budget_hours: 30\n201\t    deps: [plan_005]\n202\t    metrics:\n203\t      - cross_backbone_phase0_f1\n204\t      - cross_backbone_gain_over_baseline\n205\t\n206\t  - id: plan_012\n207\t    claim: 2\n208\t    title: \"LoRA layer placement ablation\"\n209\t    description: >\n210\t      Vary which PaliGemma layers receive LoRA experts:\n211\t      (a) early layers only (2-6), (b) mid layers (6-12), (c) late layers (12-16),\n212\t      (d) all layers. Test hypothesis that Visual LoRA benefits from early layers,\n213\t      Spatial from mid, Physical from late.\n214\t    priority: P2\n215\t    gpu_budget_hours: 15\n216\t    deps: [plan_002]\n217\t    metrics:\n218\t      - optimal_layer_placement_per_expert_type\n219\t\n220\t# ═══════════════ Budget Summary ═══════════════\n221\t# P0 total: 100 GPU-hrs (plan_001: 5, plan_002: 55, plan_003: 15, plan_005: 25, plan_007: 0)\n222\t# P1 total: 25 GPU-hrs (plan_004: 5, plan_006: 10, plan_008: 10)\n223\t# P2 total: 65 GPU-hrs (plan_009: 5, plan_010: 15, plan_011: 30, plan_012: 15)\n224\t# Grand total: 190 GPU-hrs (within budget, adjusted for RTX PRO 6000)\n225\t# Available: 4x RTX PRO 6000 96GB @ westd-34142\n226\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TS7ZtYzArmC2BQVRofu5TC","session_id":"1f5e0932-32f2-4b4b-9817-0d2ade2fda2a","uuid":"1d3894ea-d9e4-4da1-af8e-d6b41dc29c22","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:29:36.783Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a0d4bbc312a4c688e","tool_use_id":"toolu_012E1WitpW7NLWrGRb4S6HGk","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":108068,"tool_uses":25,"duration_ms":152225},"uuid":"ed1257b8-6c1f-44d2-aa61-fb240d685f3b","session_id":"1f5e0932-32f2-4b4b-9817-0d2ade2fda2a"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012E1WitpW7NLWrGRb4S6HGk","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have comprehensive understanding of the paper, its experimental design, results, and the underlying project context. Let me also quickly check the memory note about the inconsistency between first-token and mean pooling.\n\nThe paper states the PAR uses \"mean-pooled layer-6 activations\" (line 96 of paper.tex), but the memory.md and decision D006 record that first-token pooling was chosen with F1=1.000, superior to mean pooling (0.978). This is a direct contradiction within the materials.\n\nNow let me read the experiment plan's mention of the random routing baseline, which appears in plan_005 but is absent from the paper.\n\nI have thoroughly reviewed all materials. Here is my review.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper proposes Meta-Brain, a modular framework for improving VLA robustness under environmental perturbations. The core insight is that frozen VLA internal representations (PaliGemma layer 6) already encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy (F1=0.993), enabling a lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR, 48K params) to route inputs to type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual, Spatial, Physical). On LIBERO-Plus, Meta-Brain achieves 78.4% with 6.1M params, gaining +13.6% over universal LoRA (p<0.001). With optional Correction Memory, it reaches 80.8%, closing 90% of the gap to VLA-GSE (81.2%, 114M params) at 19x fewer trainable parameters. Cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA shows consistent gain patterns.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1: Well-motivated architecture with clear empirical grounding.** The Phase 0 diagnosis finding (F1=0.993 from a single frozen layer) is a genuinely interesting empirical contribution that provides a clean mechanistic foundation for the routing approach. The fact that perturbation type is already linearly separable in VLA internal representations is a non-obvious finding that other work (SAFE, VLA-GSE) did not exploit for targeted correction.\n\n**S2: Thorough ablation ladder with appropriate statistical methodology.** The progression Frozen (53.6) -> Universal LoRA (64.8) -> Meta-Brain (78.4) -> Oracle (79.6) is convincing. Each step is well-motivated: the gain from routing (13.6%) is comparable to the gain from LoRA adaptation itself (11.2%), establishing routing as a primary rather than auxiliary contribution. The 5-seed evaluation with paired t-tests (p<0.001 for the key comparison) and the non-significant oracle gap (p=0.14) are appropriate. The decomposition that routing contributes 52% of the total gain is a clear and compelling narrative.\n\n**S3: Expert cross-evaluation matrix provides strong evidence for specialization.** Table 1 is one of the most informative experiments in the paper. The symmetric pattern -- experts gaining +12-20% on own types while degrading up to -20% on off-types compared to frozen baseline -- directly demonstrates that the three perturbation categories require fundamentally incompatible corrections. This is the key justification for routing over uniform adaptation.\n\n**S4: Comprehensive ablation coverage.** The paper includes 11 experiments spanning expert specialization, ablation ladder, oracle routing, FTM comparison, parameter efficiency, CM ablation, router architecture, routing granularity, cross-backbone validation, and layer placement. This is unusually thorough for a 9-page paper. Each ablation addresses a distinct reviewer concern, and results are internally consistent.\n\n**S5: The layer placement ablation (Tables 12-13) provides mechanistic insight.** The diagonal pattern (LoRA-V peaks early, LoRA-S peaks mid, LoRA-P peaks late) is consistent with the mechanistic hypothesis about how different perturbation types disrupt different processing stages. This goes beyond just showing the method works to explaining why the routing dimension aligns with the model's internal information flow.\n\n**S6: Cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA is valuable.** Demonstrating consistent gain patterns on a different architecture (SigLIP + Llama-2-7B vs PaliGemma + Gemma) strengthens the claim that perturbation diagnosability is a general property of VLA representations, not an artifact of a specific architecture.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major): The parameter comparison between Meta-Brain and Universal LoRA is unfair -- Meta-Brain uses 3x the parameters.** The ablation ladder compares Universal LoRA (~2M params) against Meta-Brain (~6.1M params). Meta-Brain uses three rank-16 LoRA experts, each ~2M params. The paper claims the gain comes from routing, but it could partially come from simply having 3x more parameters. The critical missing control is a **rank-48 universal LoRA** (~6M params, matching Meta-Brain's total parameter budget). The paper's own analysis in the imagined experiment rationale acknowledges this: \"rank-48 universal LoRA (matching 3x rank-16 param count) is predicted to saturate at ~67%\" -- but this prediction is never experimentally verified. Without this control, Claim 2 (\"targeted > uniform correction\") is confounded by a 3x parameter advantage.\n\n**W2 (Major): The random routing baseline from plan_005 is missing from the paper.** The experiment plan explicitly includes random routing as a critical control: \"If random [routing] is approximately equal to Meta-Brain, Claim 2 is falsified.\" This is the correct ensemble control -- it tests whether the routing signal matters or whether simply having three experts (any three) provides the improvement. The paper omits this entirely. A random routing baseline achieving, say, 73% would substantially weaken the routing contribution claim (reducing it from +13.6% to +5.4% attributable to routing). This is a significant omission.\n\n**W3 (Major): Cross-backbone comparison with VLA-GSE is confounded and the framing overstates its significance.** The headline claim \"19x fewer parameters\" compares Meta-Brain on pi0 (PaliGemma + Gemma) to VLA-GSE on Qwen3-VL-4B. These are fundamentally different backbone architectures with different frozen baselines. While the paper acknowledges this in the footnote of Table 7, the abstract and conclusion present \"closing 90% of the gap to VLA-GSE at 19x fewer parameters\" as a primary result. The gain-over-frozen metric partially addresses this, but the comparison remains weak because: (a) VLA-GSE's frozen baseline of 53.6% on a much larger model (4B vs ~3B for pi0) may imply the model has more headroom for correction, and (b) the perturbation-level performance profile is likely very different across backbones. This comparison should be explicitly framed as indirect/suggestive rather than as a core claim.\n\n**W4 (Moderate): Results are all even numbers, raising questions about precision/granularity.** Every single success rate in every table is an integer multiple of 2.0 (42, 44, 46, 48, 50, 52, 54, 56, 58, 60, 62, 64, 66, 68, 70, 72, 74, 76, 78, 80, 82, 86, 88, 90). This is suspicious -- with 5 seeds and presumably dozens of evaluation episodes per perturbation type per seed, one would expect non-round numbers. This pattern suggests either (a) extremely low evaluation episode counts (e.g., 10 episodes per condition per seed, where each episode contributes 10%), (b) a modeling artifact in how results were generated, or (c) heavy rounding. If (a), the statistical power of the 5-seed analysis is questionable. The paper should report the exact number of evaluation episodes per condition.\n\n**W5 (Moderate): The paper text contradicts the project decisions on PAR pooling strategy.** The method section (line 96) states PAR uses \"mean-pooled layer-6 activations,\" but the project's decision log (D006) and memory document record that first-token pooling was adopted because it achieved F1=1.000 vs mean pooling's 0.978. This internal inconsistency should be reconciled. If first-token pooling is indeed used (as the decision record suggests), the paper should reflect this.\n\n**W6 (Moderate): LIBERO-Plus evaluation is single-benchmark, sim-only.** While the paper correctly notes that LIBERO-Plus is the standard benchmark and sim-only evaluation is the accepted pattern, evaluating on a single simulation benchmark limits the generalizability claims. The perturbation taxonomy (3 classes) is specifically designed for LIBERO-Plus's 6 perturbation types; it is unclear whether this taxonomy would generalize to other environments with different perturbation structures.\n\n**W7 (Minor): The Correction Memory module adds architectural complexity for modest gain.** CM contributes +2.4% with 1 learnable parameter and a FAISS index. While presented as optional, it occupies significant space in the method section (Sec 3.2) and results (Sec 4.7). The benefit is within noise for most perturbation types. More importantly, CM requires storing successful rollout activations, which introduces a deployment assumption (access to a curated exemplar bank) that the core Meta-Brain system does not require.\n\n**W8 (Minor): The \"overall\" success rate computation is unclear.** LIBERO-Plus has 4 visual subtypes, 1 spatial subtype, and 1 physical subtype. If overall is a simple average across all 6 subtypes, it is heavily biased toward visual perturbations (4/6 weight). If it is weighted by category (Visual/Spatial/Physical each 1/3), the computation should be stated. From the numbers: (42+58+52+46+68+56)/6 = 53.67 approximately 53.6, confirming it is a simple average, which gives visual perturbations 4/6 weight.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1:** What is the performance of a parameter-matched universal LoRA (rank-48, ~6M params)? This is the single most important missing control for validating Claim 2. Without it, the 13.6% gain over universal LoRA could be partially attributed to a 3x parameter advantage rather than routing.\n\n**Q2:** What is the performance of random routing (randomly assigning one of the three LoRA experts per episode)? This was explicitly planned in your experiment design (plan_005) as a falsification test for Claim 2 but is absent from the paper.\n\n**Q3:** How many evaluation episodes are used per perturbation type per seed? The uniformly even success rates suggest a small number of episodes (possibly 50 or 100 per condition). Please provide the exact evaluation protocol.\n\n**Q4:** Does the PAR use mean pooling or first-token pooling? The paper says mean pooling (Sec 3.2), but your Phase 0 analysis found first-token pooling was superior and decided to adopt it (Decision D006). Which is actually used in the reported experiments?\n\n**Q5:** How is the overall success rate computed -- simple average across 6 perturbation types or weighted by category? Given that visual types constitute 4/6 of the subtypes, this weighting significantly affects the overall numbers and comparisons.\n\n**Q6:** The LoRA experts are \"trained independently on type-specific data then frozen.\" What happens when the perturbation type is incorrectly classified at deployment? Is there a fallback mechanism for out-of-distribution or ambiguous perturbations? The paper mentions PAR outputs a soft distribution, but how does the system behave when routing entropy is high?\n\n**Q7:** For the cross-backbone OpenVLA experiment, are the LoRA experts also rank-16? Is the total parameter count comparable? What is the specific LoRA configuration on OpenVLA's SigLIP encoder vs Llama-2-7B backbone?\n\n---\n\n### Missing Experiments\n\n**M1 (Critical): Parameter-matched universal LoRA baseline (rank-48, ~6M params).** This isolates the routing contribution from the parameter count advantage and is essential for Claim 2.\n\n**M2 (Critical): Random routing baseline.** This was planned in plan_005 but omitted. It controls for ensemble effects and is the primary falsification test for whether routing matters.\n\n**M3 (Important): Evaluation on unperturbed (clean) LIBERO tasks.** The paper reports only perturbed performance. A robustness method should not degrade clean performance. If Meta-Brain's routing activates LoRA experts on clean data (which has no perturbation type), it could hurt performance. This is a standard control for any robustness paper.\n\n**M4 (Important): Latency/throughput analysis.** The paper claims \"zero-overhead routing\" and \"single forward pass,\" but does not report wall-clock inference latency. Adding a routing MLP, computing all three LoRA expert outputs (since soft routing requires all three), and optionally performing FAISS retrieval adds overhead. Quantifying this is important for the real-time robot control context.\n\n**M5 (Desirable): Mixed/compound perturbation evaluation.** LIBERO-Plus tests perturbation types in isolation. Real-world deployment often involves multiple simultaneous perturbations (e.g., changed lighting AND repositioned objects). How does the soft routing mechanism handle compound perturbations? The 8% \"routing leakage\" mentioned in the analysis is presented as beneficial for mixed cases, but this is never tested.\n\n**M6 (Desirable): Per-seed variance reporting.** While 5 seeds are used and standard deviations are reported for the overall ablation ladder (Table 3), the per-perturbation breakdowns (Tables 1, 2, 4-13) do not report variance. Given that individual perturbation types may have fewer evaluation episodes, the per-type numbers could have high variance.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The bibliography is incomplete -- several cited works (MoIRA, CORAL, LoRA-SP, AnyCamVLA, AFI, SRPO, CorridorVLA) appear in the related work text but have no bib entries.\n\n2. The paper uses `\\cite{vla-gse}` for \"VLA-GSE\" but the bib entry (line 523) lists it as a CoRL 2026 paper. If this paper is also submitted to CoRL 2026, the authors should clarify whether VLA-GSE is a concurrent submission or a published work.\n\n3. Table 7 uses the asterisk notation for VLA-GSE's different backbone, but the table note says \"gain is over its own frozen baseline.\" However, VLA-GSE's frozen baseline is stated as 53.6%, which coincidentally matches pi0's frozen baseline. This coincidence should be noted or the actual VLA-GSE baseline should be cited precisely.\n\n4. The paper claims \"Meta-Brain achieves 78.4% with 6.1M trainable parameters\" but elsewhere states \"each expert contributes ~2M parameters\" and there are 3 experts + 48K PAR = 6.048M. The 6.1M figure should be reconciled or the extra 52K parameters explained.\n\n5. Section 4.2 claims \"cross-type degradation up to -20% vs frozen baseline\" but checking Table 1: LoRA-P on camera = 40.0, Frozen on camera = 42.0, delta = -2.0, not -20. The -20 appears to refer to the gap between specialist on own-type minus off-type, not relative to frozen baseline. The text is misleading.\n\n6. The paper does not describe the E2E training objective for PAR. It says \"optimizing task success as the objective\" (line 123-124) but does not specify whether this is a policy gradient / REINFORCE signal, a differentiable proxy, or something else. How does gradient flow through the soft routing weights to the PAR MLP?\n\n7. In Table 6 (FTM comparison), the text states \"FTM achieves 71.5% average on visual subtypes\" but (70+76+72+68)/4 = 71.5. However, the text then says \"(close to LoRA-V's 76.0%)\" -- LoRA-V's visual average from Table 1 is (74+80+78+72)/4 = 76.0. The gap is 4.5pp, not negligible, and \"close to\" is somewhat misleading.\n\n8. The contribution framing claims \"a systematic design-dimension analysis\" as contribution 3, but the paper does not present a formal analysis framework for comparing routing dimensions -- the comparison with VLA-GSE is done through a single parameter efficiency table rather than a systematic analysis of when perturbation-diagnostic vs capacity-based routing is preferred."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a0d4bbc312a4c688e (use SendMessage with to: 'a0d4bbc312a4c688e' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 111111\ntool_uses: 25\nduration_ms: 152227</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f5e0932-32f2-4b4b-9817-0d2ade2fda2a","uuid":"3d5fb78f-71b2-4c8d-a364-c6da1143cf96","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:31:07.518Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. Produce a detailed, independent review. Do NOT suggest an overall score yet — that comes later in the roundtable.\n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal; 9=strong accept top 15%; 8=clear accept top 50%; 7=good paper accept; 6=marginally above threshold; 5=marginally below; 4=not good enough reject; 3=clear reject; 2=strong reject; 1=trivial/wrong)\n- Acceptance rate: ~30-35%\n- Key criteria: significance/novelty of results (theoretical or empirical), objective establishment of results, potential for scientific/technological impact, relevance to robot learning\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic Routing of LoRA Experts for Parameter-Efficient VLA Robustness\"\n\nThe paper proposes Meta-Brain, a modular framework for improving VLA robustness under environmental perturbations. Key idea: VLA internal representations already encode perturbation type (linear probe on PaliGemma layer 6 achieves F1=0.993). A lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR, 48K params) diagnoses perturbation type and routes to one of three type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual, Spatial, Physical). On LIBERO-Plus, Meta-Brain achieves 78.4% with 6.1M params, +24.8% over frozen baseline, +13.6% over universal LoRA (p&lt;0.001). With optional Correction Memory (CM), reaches 80.8%, closing 90% of gap to VLA-GSE (81.2%, 114M params) at 19x fewer params. Cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA confirms generalizability (+11.8% over universal LoRA).\n\nThree claims:\n1. Perturbation diagnosability (F1=0.993 from single layer)\n2. Targeted > uniform correction (+13.6%, p&lt;0.001)\n3. Parameter-efficient robustness (19x fewer params vs VLA-GSE)\n\n## Paper Structure (9 pages, 13 tables)\n- Section 1: Introduction — three claims, contributions\n- Section 2: Related Work — VLA robustness (LIBERO-Plus, SAFE, VLA-GSE, RobustVLA), parameter-efficient adaptation (LoRA, MoIRA, CORAL, LoRA-SP, FTM/FLA)\n- Section 3: Method\n  - 3.1 Phase 0: Perturbation Diagnosis (linear probe on PaliGemma layer 6, 3-class F1=0.993)\n  - 3.2 Phase 1: Meta-Brain Architecture (PAR 48K params, ALB 3 frozen LoRA experts rank-16, CM optional with FAISS)\n  - Training: LoRA experts trained independently on type-specific data then frozen; only PAR fine-tuned E2E\n- Section 4: Experiments\n  - 4.1 Setup: LIBERO-Plus, 6 perturbation types, pi0 backbone, 5 seeds\n  - 4.2 Expert Specialization (Table 1: cross-eval matrix, Table 2: specialization delta +12-20%)\n  - 4.3 Ablation Ladder (Table 3: Frozen 53.6 → Universal 64.8 → Meta-Brain 78.4 → Oracle 79.6; Table 4: per-perturbation)\n  - 4.4 Oracle Routing (Table 5: PAR-Oracle gap 1.2%)\n  - 4.5 FTM Comparison (Table 6: FTM 68.3% overall, visual-only; Meta-Brain +10.1%)\n  - 4.6 Parameter Efficiency (Table 7: gain/M-param comparison; VLA-GSE on different backbone*)\n  - 4.7 CM Ablation (Table 8: +2.4% gain, noise +4.0%)\n  - 4.8 Router Architecture (Table 9: Linear 76.6, MLP 78.4, Attention 78.8)\n  - 4.9 6-class vs 3-class (Table 10: +1.0% at 2x params, robot -4.0%)\n  - 4.10 Cross-backbone OpenVLA (Table 11: gain over universal +11.8%, F1=0.972)\n  - 4.11 Layer Placement (Tables 12-13: V-early, S-mid, P-late diagonal; type-aligned +1.8%)\n- Section 5: Discussion — routing > capacity argument, easy diagnosis ≠ trivial, limitations\n- Section 6: Conclusion\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (review_context.yaml)\nThe imagined experiment plan includes these experiments:\n1. plan_002: LoRA expert training (3 specialized + 1 universal) → Table 1-2\n2. plan_003: Meta-Brain assembly PAR+ALB E2E → Tables 3-4\n3. plan_004: Oracle routing ablation → Table 5\n4. plan_005: Ablation ladder 5 seeds → Table 3\n5. plan_006: FTM/FLA reproduction → Table 6\n6. plan_007: Parameter efficiency analysis → Table 7\n7. plan_008: Correction Memory ablation → Table 8\n8. plan_009: Router architecture comparison → Table 9\n9. plan_010: 6-class vs 3-class → Table 10\n10. plan_011: Cross-backbone OpenVLA → Table 11\n11. plan_012: LoRA layer placement → Tables 12-13\n\nAll imagined experiments appear to be covered in the paper.\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Method soundness**: Is the Phase 0 → Phase 1 pipeline technically sound? Is the PAR router well-motivated? Is the soft routing formulation appropriate? Is the CM module well-designed?\n2. **Experimental rigor**: Are the experiments well-designed? Are baselines fair? Is the statistical methodology appropriate (5 seeds, paired t-tests)? Are there confounds?\n3. **Result interpretation**: Are claims supported by the evidence? Is the ablation ladder convincing? Is the parameter efficiency comparison fair (VLA-GSE uses different backbone)?\n4. **Missing experiments / controls**: What experiments are missing that would strengthen the paper?\n5. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters specified?\n\nStructure your review as:\n### Summary\n### Strengths (S1, S2, ...)\n### Weaknesses (W1, W2, ...)\n### Questions for Authors (Q1, Q2, ...)\n### Missing Experiments\n### Minor Issues\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Identify both genuine weaknesses and genuine strengths.","agentId":"a0d4bbc312a4c688e","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have comprehensive understanding of the paper, its experimental design, results, and the underlying project context. Let me also quickly check the memory note about the inconsistency between first-token and mean pooling.\n\nThe paper states the PAR uses \"mean-pooled layer-6 activations\" (line 96 of paper.tex), but the memory.md and decision D006 record that first-token pooling was chosen with F1=1.000, superior to mean pooling (0.978). This is a direct contradiction within the materials.\n\nNow let me read the experiment plan's mention of the random routing baseline, which appears in plan_005 but is absent from the paper.\n\nI have thoroughly reviewed all materials. Here is my review.\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper proposes Meta-Brain, a modular framework for improving VLA robustness under environmental perturbations. The core insight is that frozen VLA internal representations (PaliGemma layer 6) already encode perturbation type with near-perfect accuracy (F1=0.993), enabling a lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR, 48K params) to route inputs to type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual, Spatial, Physical). On LIBERO-Plus, Meta-Brain achieves 78.4% with 6.1M params, gaining +13.6% over universal LoRA (p<0.001). With optional Correction Memory, it reaches 80.8%, closing 90% of the gap to VLA-GSE (81.2%, 114M params) at 19x fewer trainable parameters. Cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA shows consistent gain patterns.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1: Well-motivated architecture with clear empirical grounding.** The Phase 0 diagnosis finding (F1=0.993 from a single frozen layer) is a genuinely interesting empirical contribution that provides a clean mechanistic foundation for the routing approach. The fact that perturbation type is already linearly separable in VLA internal representations is a non-obvious finding that other work (SAFE, VLA-GSE) did not exploit for targeted correction.\n\n**S2: Thorough ablation ladder with appropriate statistical methodology.** The progression Frozen (53.6) -> Universal LoRA (64.8) -> Meta-Brain (78.4) -> Oracle (79.6) is convincing. Each step is well-motivated: the gain from routing (13.6%) is comparable to the gain from LoRA adaptation itself (11.2%), establishing routing as a primary rather than auxiliary contribution. The 5-seed evaluation with paired t-tests (p<0.001 for the key comparison) and the non-significant oracle gap (p=0.14) are appropriate. The decomposition that routing contributes 52% of the total gain is a clear and compelling narrative.\n\n**S3: Expert cross-evaluation matrix provides strong evidence for specialization.** Table 1 is one of the most informative experiments in the paper. The symmetric pattern -- experts gaining +12-20% on own types while degrading up to -20% on off-types compared to frozen baseline -- directly demonstrates that the three perturbation categories require fundamentally incompatible corrections. This is the key justification for routing over uniform adaptation.\n\n**S4: Comprehensive ablation coverage.** The paper includes 11 experiments spanning expert specialization, ablation ladder, oracle routing, FTM comparison, parameter efficiency, CM ablation, router architecture, routing granularity, cross-backbone validation, and layer placement. This is unusually thorough for a 9-page paper. Each ablation addresses a distinct reviewer concern, and results are internally consistent.\n\n**S5: The layer placement ablation (Tables 12-13) provides mechanistic insight.** The diagonal pattern (LoRA-V peaks early, LoRA-S peaks mid, LoRA-P peaks late) is consistent with the mechanistic hypothesis about how different perturbation types disrupt different processing stages. This goes beyond just showing the method works to explaining why the routing dimension aligns with the model's internal information flow.\n\n**S6: Cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA is valuable.** Demonstrating consistent gain patterns on a different architecture (SigLIP + Llama-2-7B vs PaliGemma + Gemma) strengthens the claim that perturbation diagnosability is a general property of VLA representations, not an artifact of a specific architecture.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major): The parameter comparison between Meta-Brain and Universal LoRA is unfair -- Meta-Brain uses 3x the parameters.** The ablation ladder compares Universal LoRA (~2M params) against Meta-Brain (~6.1M params). Meta-Brain uses three rank-16 LoRA experts, each ~2M params. The paper claims the gain comes from routing, but it could partially come from simply having 3x more parameters. The critical missing control is a **rank-48 universal LoRA** (~6M params, matching Meta-Brain's total parameter budget). The paper's own analysis in the imagined experiment rationale acknowledges this: \"rank-48 universal LoRA (matching 3x rank-16 param count) is predicted to saturate at ~67%\" -- but this prediction is never experimentally verified. Without this control, Claim 2 (\"targeted > uniform correction\") is confounded by a 3x parameter advantage.\n\n**W2 (Major): The random routing baseline from plan_005 is missing from the paper.** The experiment plan explicitly includes random routing as a critical control: \"If random [routing] is approximately equal to Meta-Brain, Claim 2 is falsified.\" This is the correct ensemble control -- it tests whether the routing signal matters or whether simply having three experts (any three) provides the improvement. The paper omits this entirely. A random routing baseline achieving, say, 73% would substantially weaken the routing contribution claim (reducing it from +13.6% to +5.4% attributable to routing). This is a significant omission.\n\n**W3 (Major): Cross-backbone comparison with VLA-GSE is confounded and the framing overstates its significance.** The headline claim \"19x fewer parameters\" compares Meta-Brain on pi0 (PaliGemma + Gemma) to VLA-GSE on Qwen3-VL-4B. These are fundamentally different backbone architectures with different frozen baselines. While the paper acknowledges this in the footnote of Table 7, the abstract and conclusion present \"closing 90% of the gap to VLA-GSE at 19x fewer parameters\" as a primary result. The gain-over-frozen metric partially addresses this, but the comparison remains weak because: (a) VLA-GSE's frozen baseline of 53.6% on a much larger model (4B vs ~3B for pi0) may imply the model has more headroom for correction, and (b) the perturbation-level performance profile is likely very different across backbones. This comparison should be explicitly framed as indirect/suggestive rather than as a core claim.\n\n**W4 (Moderate): Results are all even numbers, raising questions about precision/granularity.** Every single success rate in every table is an integer multiple of 2.0 (42, 44, 46, 48, 50, 52, 54, 56, 58, 60, 62, 64, 66, 68, 70, 72, 74, 76, 78, 80, 82, 86, 88, 90). This is suspicious -- with 5 seeds and presumably dozens of evaluation episodes per perturbation type per seed, one would expect non-round numbers. This pattern suggests either (a) extremely low evaluation episode counts (e.g., 10 episodes per condition per seed, where each episode contributes 10%), (b) a modeling artifact in how results were generated, or (c) heavy rounding. If (a), the statistical power of the 5-seed analysis is questionable. The paper should report the exact number of evaluation episodes per condition.\n\n**W5 (Moderate): The paper text contradicts the project decisions on PAR pooling strategy.** The method section (line 96) states PAR uses \"mean-pooled layer-6 activations,\" but the project's decision log (D006) and memory document record that first-token pooling was adopted because it achieved F1=1.000 vs mean pooling's 0.978. This internal inconsistency should be reconciled. If first-token pooling is indeed used (as the decision record suggests), the paper should reflect this.\n\n**W6 (Moderate): LIBERO-Plus evaluation is single-benchmark, sim-only.** While the paper correctly notes that LIBERO-Plus is the standard benchmark and sim-only evaluation is the accepted pattern, evaluating on a single simulation benchmark limits the generalizability claims. The perturbation taxonomy (3 classes) is specifically designed for LIBERO-Plus's 6 perturbation types; it is unclear whether this taxonomy would generalize to other environments with different perturbation structures.\n\n**W7 (Minor): The Correction Memory module adds architectural complexity for modest gain.** CM contributes +2.4% with 1 learnable parameter and a FAISS index. While presented as optional, it occupies significant space in the method section (Sec 3.2) and results (Sec 4.7). The benefit is within noise for most perturbation types. More importantly, CM requires storing successful rollout activations, which introduces a deployment assumption (access to a curated exemplar bank) that the core Meta-Brain system does not require.\n\n**W8 (Minor): The \"overall\" success rate computation is unclear.** LIBERO-Plus has 4 visual subtypes, 1 spatial subtype, and 1 physical subtype. If overall is a simple average across all 6 subtypes, it is heavily biased toward visual perturbations (4/6 weight). If it is weighted by category (Visual/Spatial/Physical each 1/3), the computation should be stated. From the numbers: (42+58+52+46+68+56)/6 = 53.67 approximately 53.6, confirming it is a simple average, which gives visual perturbations 4/6 weight.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1:** What is the performance of a parameter-matched universal LoRA (rank-48, ~6M params)? This is the single most important missing control for validating Claim 2. Without it, the 13.6% gain over universal LoRA could be partially attributed to a 3x parameter advantage rather than routing.\n\n**Q2:** What is the performance of random routing (randomly assigning one of the three LoRA experts per episode)? This was explicitly planned in your experiment design (plan_005) as a falsification test for Claim 2 but is absent from the paper.\n\n**Q3:** How many evaluation episodes are used per perturbation type per seed? The uniformly even success rates suggest a small number of episodes (possibly 50 or 100 per condition). Please provide the exact evaluation protocol.\n\n**Q4:** Does the PAR use mean pooling or first-token pooling? The paper says mean pooling (Sec 3.2), but your Phase 0 analysis found first-token pooling was superior and decided to adopt it (Decision D006). Which is actually used in the reported experiments?\n\n**Q5:** How is the overall success rate computed -- simple average across 6 perturbation types or weighted by category? Given that visual types constitute 4/6 of the subtypes, this weighting significantly affects the overall numbers and comparisons.\n\n**Q6:** The LoRA experts are \"trained independently on type-specific data then frozen.\" What happens when the perturbation type is incorrectly classified at deployment? Is there a fallback mechanism for out-of-distribution or ambiguous perturbations? The paper mentions PAR outputs a soft distribution, but how does the system behave when routing entropy is high?\n\n**Q7:** For the cross-backbone OpenVLA experiment, are the LoRA experts also rank-16? Is the total parameter count comparable? What is the specific LoRA configuration on OpenVLA's SigLIP encoder vs Llama-2-7B backbone?\n\n---\n\n### Missing Experiments\n\n**M1 (Critical): Parameter-matched universal LoRA baseline (rank-48, ~6M params).** This isolates the routing contribution from the parameter count advantage and is essential for Claim 2.\n\n**M2 (Critical): Random routing baseline.** This was planned in plan_005 but omitted. It controls for ensemble effects and is the primary falsification test for whether routing matters.\n\n**M3 (Important): Evaluation on unperturbed (clean) LIBERO tasks.** The paper reports only perturbed performance. A robustness method should not degrade clean performance. If Meta-Brain's routing activates LoRA experts on clean data (which has no perturbation type), it could hurt performance. This is a standard control for any robustness paper.\n\n**M4 (Important): Latency/throughput analysis.** The paper claims \"zero-overhead routing\" and \"single forward pass,\" but does not report wall-clock inference latency. Adding a routing MLP, computing all three LoRA expert outputs (since soft routing requires all three), and optionally performing FAISS retrieval adds overhead. Quantifying this is important for the real-time robot control context.\n\n**M5 (Desirable): Mixed/compound perturbation evaluation.** LIBERO-Plus tests perturbation types in isolation. Real-world deployment often involves multiple simultaneous perturbations (e.g., changed lighting AND repositioned objects). How does the soft routing mechanism handle compound perturbations? The 8% \"routing leakage\" mentioned in the analysis is presented as beneficial for mixed cases, but this is never tested.\n\n**M6 (Desirable): Per-seed variance reporting.** While 5 seeds are used and standard deviations are reported for the overall ablation ladder (Table 3), the per-perturbation breakdowns (Tables 1, 2, 4-13) do not report variance. Given that individual perturbation types may have fewer evaluation episodes, the per-type numbers could have high variance.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The bibliography is incomplete -- several cited works (MoIRA, CORAL, LoRA-SP, AnyCamVLA, AFI, SRPO, CorridorVLA) appear in the related work text but have no bib entries.\n\n2. The paper uses `\\cite{vla-gse}` for \"VLA-GSE\" but the bib entry (line 523) lists it as a CoRL 2026 paper. If this paper is also submitted to CoRL 2026, the authors should clarify whether VLA-GSE is a concurrent submission or a published work.\n\n3. Table 7 uses the asterisk notation for VLA-GSE's different backbone, but the table note says \"gain is over its own frozen baseline.\" However, VLA-GSE's frozen baseline is stated as 53.6%, which coincidentally matches pi0's frozen baseline. This coincidence should be noted or the actual VLA-GSE baseline should be cited precisely.\n\n4. The paper claims \"Meta-Brain achieves 78.4% with 6.1M trainable parameters\" but elsewhere states \"each expert contributes ~2M parameters\" and there are 3 experts + 48K PAR = 6.048M. The 6.1M figure should be reconciled or the extra 52K parameters explained.\n\n5. Section 4.2 claims \"cross-type degradation up to -20% vs frozen baseline\" but checking Table 1: LoRA-P on camera = 40.0, Frozen on camera = 42.0, delta = -2.0, not -20. The -20 appears to refer to the gap between specialist on own-type minus off-type, not relative to frozen baseline. The text is misleading.\n\n6. The paper does not describe the E2E training objective for PAR. It says \"optimizing task success as the objective\" (line 123-124) but does not specify whether this is a policy gradient / REINFORCE signal, a differentiable proxy, or something else. How does gradient flow through the soft routing weights to the PAR MLP?\n\n7. In Table 6 (FTM comparison), the text states \"FTM achieves 71.5% average on visual subtypes\" but (70+76+72+68)/4 = 71.5. However, the text then says \"(close to LoRA-V's 76.0%)\" -- LoRA-V's visual average from Table 1 is (74+80+78+72)/4 = 76.0. The gap is 4.5pp, not negligible, and \"close to\" is somewhat misleading.\n\n8. The contribution framing claims \"a systematic design-dimension analysis\" as contribution 3, but the paper does not present a formal analysis framework for comparing routing dimensions -- the comparison with VLA-GSE is done through a single parameter efficiency table rather than a systematic analysis of when perturbation-diagnostic vs capacity-based routing is preferred."}],"totalDurationMs":152227,"totalTokens":111111,"totalToolUseCount":25,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15486,"cache_read_input_tokens":91671,"output_tokens":3953,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":15486},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3953,"cache_read_input_tokens":91671,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15486,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":15486,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":6,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":16}}}
