{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TS7ZtYzArmC2BQVRofu5TC","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper and all project context. Let me produce the review.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper proposes Meta-Brain, a modular framework that diagnoses perturbation type from frozen VLA internal activations (via a linear probe on PaliGemma layer 6, F1=0.993) and routes to one of three type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual, Spatial, Physical) for targeted correction. The system achieves 78.4% on LIBERO-Plus with 6.1M trainable parameters, claiming +13.6% over universal LoRA and 19x fewer parameters than VLA-GSE (81.2%, 114M params). Cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA is provided.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1: The core insight is well-motivated and empirically grounded.** The observation that VLA activations encode perturbation type at a single layer with near-perfect accuracy (F1=0.993) is a genuinely interesting empirical finding. The paper leverages this finding architecturally rather than just reporting it, which is the right approach. The \"close the loop\" framing -- SAFE detects but does not correct, Meta-Brain detects *and* corrects -- is a clean positioning.\n\n**S2: The experimental design is thorough within its scope.** The ablation ladder (Frozen -> Universal -> Meta-Brain -> Oracle) is well-constructed and the decomposition of the 26% total gain into routing contribution (13.6%, 52%) vs. LoRA contribution (11.2%, 43%) vs. headroom (1.2%, 5%) is informative. The oracle routing ablation (1.2% gap, p=0.14 n.s.) effectively demonstrates that the learned router is near-ceiling. The cross-evaluation matrix (Table 1) persuasively shows that expert corrections are type-specific and not simply additive robustness.\n\n**S3: The cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA strengthens the generality claim.** Demonstrating consistent gain patterns (+11.8% over universal LoRA) on a different VLA architecture (SigLIP + Llama-2-7B vs. PaliGemma + Gemma) goes beyond what most single-paper contributions provide. The fact that the best probe layer shifts (6 -> 8) while the overall mechanism transfers is a useful finding.\n\n**S4: The paper is well-structured and the narrative is coherent.** The paper flows logically from diagnosis to architecture to experiments. The 13 tables, while dense, each serves a clear purpose in the ablation hierarchy. The writing is generally clear and the claims are stated precisely.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major): Limited novelty -- the contribution is a straightforward composition of well-known components.** The core architecture is: (1) a linear probe for classification (standard since [Alain & Bengio 2017]), (2) soft routing to multiple LoRA modules (standard MoE/MoLoRA pattern), and (3) independently-trained LoRA experts (standard PEFT). None of these components is novel individually, and their composition follows a natural design pattern that any practitioner familiar with both MoE and LoRA would arrive at. The specific instantiation -- routing by *perturbation type* rather than by task or token -- is a valid design choice but represents a modest conceptual step. The paper frames this as introducing a \"new routing dimension,\" but the routing mechanism itself (MLP on intermediate features -> softmax -> weighted sum of experts) is identical to standard MoE gating. The novelty lies in *what signal* drives routing (perturbation type), not in *how* routing is done. This is an application insight, not a methodological advance.\n\n**W2 (Major): Severely incomplete related work with missing citations for directly relevant methods.** The paper mentions MoIRA, CORAL, and LoRA-SP *by name* in Section 2 but provides no citations -- this is a significant omission for a published paper. More critically, the paper fails to cite or discuss several lines of work that are directly relevant:\n\n- **Mixture-of-LoRA-Experts literature**: LoRAMoE [Dou et al., 2024], MoLoRA [Zadouri et al., 2023], MixLoRA [Li et al., 2024], MOELoRA [Liu et al., 2023], and LoRA-Switch [Mok et al., 2024] all explore routing among multiple LoRA modules. Some of these use soft routing with gating functions very similar to PAR. The paper must explain how Meta-Brain differs from these methods beyond the choice of routing signal.\n\n- **Domain-conditional / context-conditional adaptation**: AdapterFusion [Pfeiffer et al., 2021], Poly [Ponti et al., 2023], and related work on composing adapters conditioned on input characteristics predate this work and share the same design pattern.\n\n- **Test-time adaptation / domain detection + correction**: Test-time training (TTT) [Sun et al., 2020] and TENT [Wang et al., 2021] perform online adaptation at test time based on detected distribution shifts. The relationship to these methods should be discussed -- Meta-Brain can be viewed as a discrete version of test-time adaptation where the \"adaptation\" is pre-computed.\n\n- **Failure diagnosis in robotics**: The robotics community has extensive work on failure detection, diagnosis, and recovery (e.g., Inceoglu et al., 2021; Di Lello et al., 2013). The paper should contextualize its perturbation diagnosis within this broader tradition.\n\n- **PaliGemma probing**: The paper should cite the original PaliGemma work [Beyer et al., 2024] separately from pi0, and the broader literature on probing representations for distribution shift detection (e.g., Guillory et al., 2021).\n\nWith only 8 references in total, the bibliography is remarkably thin for a venue like CoRL. A typical CoRL paper cites 30-50 works.\n\n**W3 (Major): Unfair parameter comparison with VLA-GSE masks the true competitive picture.** The headline claim -- \"19x fewer parameters than VLA-GSE\" -- compares across different backbones (pi0/PaliGemma vs. Qwen3-VL-4B). This is acknowledged in a footnote but nonetheless dominates the abstract, introduction, and conclusion. The comparison is misleading because: (a) the backbone sizes differ substantially (PaliGemma ~3B vs. Qwen3-VL-4B ~4B), making the ratio of trainable-to-total parameters different; (b) the absolute performance gap (78.4% vs. 81.2%) cannot be attributed to the method vs. the backbone; (c) the \"gain-over-frozen\" normalization used to make the comparison fairer (24.8 vs. 27.6) still compares gains on fundamentally different systems. A fair comparison would require running VLA-GSE's MoE routing on the same pi0 backbone, or running Meta-Brain on Qwen3-VL-4B. The OpenVLA cross-backbone result partially addresses generalizability but does not resolve the apples-to-oranges VLA-GSE comparison.\n\n**W4 (Major): The three \"contributions\" are really one contribution repackaged.** Contribution 1 (diagnosability) is a prerequisite observation. Contribution 2 (the architecture) is the system. Contribution 3 (parameter efficiency) is a consequence of the architecture design. These are not independent contributions -- they are the setup, the method, and a property of the method, respectively. Presenting them as three distinct contributions inflates the paper's apparent contribution scope. A more honest framing would be: \"We contribute Meta-Brain, a system that exploits the diagnosability of perturbation types (which we empirically establish) to achieve parameter-efficient routing of corrective LoRA experts.\"\n\n**W5 (Moderate): The routing setting is arguably too easy to be interesting.** The Phase 0 finding (F1=0.993, and F1=1.000 with first-token pooling at K=1) is presented as a strength, but it raises a question: if perturbation types are so trivially separable in the frozen representation space, does this mean that a universal LoRA with sufficient capacity (e.g., rank-48 instead of rank-16) could learn to handle all types without explicit routing? The paper compares Meta-Brain (3 x rank-16 = ~6M) against a rank-16 universal LoRA (~2M), but critically **never compares against a rank-48 universal LoRA (~6M)** that would match the total parameter budget. The claimed \"routing vs. uniform\" comparison confounds routing benefit with parameter count (6M vs. 2M). The paper acknowledges this in the imagined experiments (\"rank-48 universal LoRA should still saturate at ~67%\") but this experiment is not actually run. Without this control, Claim 2 is not properly established.\n\n**W6 (Moderate): Simulation-only evaluation is a limitation, though standard for the venue.** The paper correctly notes that SAFE, VLA-GSE, and RobustVLA are also sim-only. However, the \"perturbation type is known\" assumption is much stronger in simulation (where perturbation type is a controlled variable) than in reality (where multiple perturbations may co-occur, perturbation type may be ambiguous, or perturbations may be continuous rather than categorical). The 3-class taxonomy is a clean decomposition of LIBERO-Plus's controlled perturbation set, but it is unclear how this taxonomy would transfer to unstructured real-world perturbations where boundaries between \"visual\" and \"spatial\" are blurred.\n\n**W7 (Minor): The Correction Memory module adds complexity without sufficient justification.** CM adds 2.4% over Meta-Brain. While presented as \"optional,\" it occupies significant paper real estate (a dedicated subsection and table). The mechanism (FAISS k-NN retrieval modulating routing weights) is not novel and the gain is modest. This space would be better used for the missing parameter-matched baseline (W5) or expanded related work (W2).\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1:** Can you run a rank-48 universal LoRA (matching Meta-Brain's total ~6M parameter budget) as a baseline? Without this, the claim that routing outperforms uniform adaptation is confounded by parameter count.\n\n**Q2:** How does Meta-Brain handle mixed perturbations -- e.g., simultaneous camera angle change + layout shift? LIBERO-Plus applies perturbations individually; does the system degrade when multiple perturbation types co-occur? The soft routing mechanism could in principle handle this (assigning partial weight to multiple experts), but this is never tested.\n\n**Q3:** Why are MoIRA, CORAL, and LoRA-SP mentioned without citations? Are these concurrent or unpublished works? If they are published, citations are required. If they are internal names, they should not appear in the paper.\n\n**Q4:** The paper states the PAR uses \"mean-pooled layer-6 activations\" (Section 3.2), but the memory.md records a decision (D006) to switch to \"first-token pooling\" because it achieves F1=1.000 vs. mean pooling's 0.978. Which pooling method is actually used in the reported experiments? This inconsistency between the paper text and the project records needs clarification.\n\n**Q5:** The training procedure uses independently trained LoRA experts that are then frozen, with only the PAR fine-tuned end-to-end. Why not fine-tune the LoRA experts jointly with the router? What is the performance gap between this staged training and true end-to-end training?\n\n**Q6:** How does the system behave on clean (unperturbed) observations? If deployed on standard LIBERO (no perturbations), does Meta-Brain maintain the >90% baseline performance, or does routing introduce degradation?\n\n**Q7:** The \"random routing\" baseline from the experiment plan (plan_005) appears in the plan but not in the paper's ablation ladder (Table 3). Why was it removed? This is a critical control that distinguishes routing benefit from ensemble benefit.\n\n---\n\n### Missing References\n\nThe following categories of work should be cited and discussed:\n\n1. **MoIRA, CORAL, LoRA-SP** -- mentioned by name in the paper without citations. This is unacceptable in a published paper.\n\n2. **Mixture-of-LoRA literature**: LoRAMoE [Dou et al., 2024], MoLoRA [Zadouri et al., 2023], MOELoRA [Liu et al., 2023], MixLoRA [Li et al., 2024], LoRA-Switch [Mok et al., 2024].\n\n3. **Adapter composition/fusion**: AdapterFusion [Pfeiffer et al., 2021], Poly [Ponti et al., 2023], AdaMix [Wang et al., 2022].\n\n4. **Test-time adaptation**: TENT [Wang et al., 2021], TTT [Sun et al., 2020], or at minimum a sentence acknowledging this related direction.\n\n5. **PaliGemma**: [Beyer et al., 2024] -- the vision encoder being probed.\n\n6. **Representation probing foundations**: [Alain & Bengio, 2017] for linear probes; [Belinkov, 2022] for probing survey.\n\n7. **MoE fundamentals**: [Shazeer et al., 2017] or [Fedus et al., 2022] -- the gating mechanism in PAR is a direct descendant.\n\n8. **LIBERO base benchmark**: [Liu et al., 2024] -- the original LIBERO should be cited alongside LIBERO-Plus.\n\nThe current 8-reference bibliography is far below CoRL norms and creates the impression that the authors have not engaged with the broader literature.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. **Inconsistency in parameter counts**: The paper framing document states \"~6M\" while the LoRA injection verification shows 3 x 3.7M = 11.1M. The paper text uses 6.1M. These need reconciliation.\n\n2. **Table 6 (FTM comparison)**: The text states \"FTM achieves 71.5% average on visual subtypes\" but this value is not in the table -- the visual average from the table numbers is (70+76+72+68)/4 = 71.5, so it is correct but should be stated explicitly as a computed average.\n\n3. **Section 3.2 states PAR uses \"mean-pooled\" features** but project decision D006 switched to first-token pooling. The paper must reflect the actual implementation.\n\n4. **No architecture figure**: The paper describes the architecture in text only. A system diagram is essential for clarity and is listed as \"planned\" in the framing document but absent from the paper.\n\n5. **No t-SNE / feature visualization**: The paper claims perturbation types are separable in activation space but provides no visualization. A t-SNE plot of layer-6 activations colored by perturbation type would be compelling evidence and is standard for probing papers.\n\n6. **The LIBERO-Plus reference is cited as CVPR 2026** -- this would be a concurrent submission. If not yet published, this should be indicated as \"to appear\" or \"under review.\"\n\n7. **The VLA-GSE reference is cited as CoRL 2026** -- same venue as this submission. If this is a concurrent submission to the same venue, the comparison requires special care (blind review considerations, inability to verify their numbers independently).\n\n8. **All results appear to use even integer percentages** (42%, 54%, 72%, etc.), which is unusual for averaged results over 5 seeds. This suggests either rounding to suspicious precision or that the results are not from actual experiments.\n\n9. **The overall weighted average for Frozen pi0 (53.6%)** does not match a simple average of the six per-perturbation values (42+58+52+46+68+56)/6 = 53.67, which rounds to 53.7. This minor discrepancy should be clarified (perhaps due to unequal episode counts per perturbation type)."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a12a43b9fe4441923 (use SendMessage with to: 'a12a43b9fe4441923' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 80231\ntool_uses: 17\nduration_ms: 150731</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f5e0932-32f2-4b4b-9817-0d2ade2fda2a","uuid":"db2fd4dd-c278-4078-ab72-10b082f15997","timestamp":"2026-05-15T16:31:26.489Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **Novelty** and **Related Work**. Produce a detailed, independent review. Do NOT suggest an overall score yet — that comes later in the roundtable.\n\n## Venue: CoRL 2026\n- Rating scale: 1-10 (10=top 5% seminal; 9=strong accept top 15%; 8=clear accept top 50%; 7=good paper accept; 6=marginally above threshold; 5=marginally below; 4=not good enough reject; 3=clear reject; 2=strong reject; 1=trivial/wrong)\n- Acceptance rate: ~30-35%\n- Key criteria: significance/novelty of results (theoretical or empirical), objective establishment of results, potential for scientific/technological impact, relevance to robot learning\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic Routing of LoRA Experts for Parameter-Efficient VLA Robustness\"\n\nThe paper proposes Meta-Brain, a modular framework for improving VLA robustness under environmental perturbations. Key idea: VLA internal representations already encode perturbation type (linear probe on PaliGemma layer 6 achieves F1=0.993). A lightweight Perturbation-Aware Router (PAR, 48K params) diagnoses perturbation type and routes to one of three type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual, Spatial, Physical). On LIBERO-Plus, Meta-Brain achieves 78.4% with 6.1M params, +24.8% over frozen baseline, +13.6% over universal LoRA (p&lt;0.001). With optional Correction Memory (CM), reaches 80.8%, closing 90% of gap to VLA-GSE (81.2%, 114M params) at 19x fewer params. Cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA confirms generalizability (+11.8% over universal LoRA).\n\nThree claims:\n1. Perturbation diagnosability (F1=0.993 from single layer)\n2. Targeted > uniform correction (+13.6%, p&lt;0.001)\n3. Parameter-efficient robustness (19x fewer params vs VLA-GSE)\n\n## Paper Structure (9 pages, 13 tables)\n- Section 1: Introduction — three claims, contributions\n- Section 2: Related Work — VLA robustness (LIBERO-Plus, SAFE, VLA-GSE, RobustVLA), parameter-efficient adaptation (LoRA, MoIRA, CORAL, LoRA-SP, FTM/FLA)\n- Section 3: Method\n  - 3.1 Phase 0: Perturbation Diagnosis (linear probe on PaliGemma layer 6, 3-class F1=0.993)\n  - 3.2 Phase 1: Meta-Brain Architecture (PAR 48K params, ALB 3 frozen LoRA experts rank-16, CM optional with FAISS)\n  - Training: LoRA experts trained independently on type-specific data then frozen; only PAR fine-tuned E2E\n- Section 4: Experiments\n  - 4.1 Setup: LIBERO-Plus, 6 perturbation types, pi0 backbone, 5 seeds\n  - 4.2 Expert Specialization (Table 1: cross-eval matrix, Table 2: specialization delta +12-20%)\n  - 4.3 Ablation Ladder (Table 3: Frozen 53.6 → Universal 64.8 → Meta-Brain 78.4 → Oracle 79.6)\n  - 4.4 Oracle Routing (Table 5: PAR-Oracle gap 1.2%)\n  - 4.5 FTM Comparison (Table 6: FTM 68.3% overall)\n  - 4.6 Parameter Efficiency (Table 7: VLA-GSE on different backbone Qwen3-VL-4B)\n  - 4.7 CM Ablation (Table 8: +2.4%)\n  - 4.8 Router Architecture (Table 9: Linear/MLP/Attention)\n  - 4.9 6-class vs 3-class (Table 10)\n  - 4.10 Cross-backbone OpenVLA (Table 11)\n  - 4.11 Layer Placement (Tables 12-13)\n- Section 5: Discussion\n- Section 6: Conclusion\n- References: 8 entries [1]-[8]\n\n## Related work cited\n1. LoRA [Hu et al., ICLR 2022]\n2. LIBERO-Plus [Liu et al., CVPR 2026]\n3. VLA-GSE [Wang et al., CoRL 2026]\n4. SAFE [Chen et al., arXiv 2025]\n5. FTM/FLA [Li et al., ICRA 2025]\n6. RobustVLA [Zhang et al., RSS 2026]\n7. pi0 [Physical Intelligence, arXiv 2024]\n8. OpenVLA [Kim et al., CoRL 2024]\n\nAlso mentioned but not cited: MoIRA, CORAL, LoRA-SP\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is the core contribution? Is \"perturbation-diagnostic routing\" a genuinely new idea or a straightforward combination of known techniques (probing + MoE routing + LoRA)? How does it compare to the rich MoE/routing literature?\n2. **Related work coverage**: Are there important missing references? The paper mentions MoIRA, CORAL, LoRA-SP without citations. Are there other relevant works in:\n   - Mixture of LoRA experts (MoLoRA, LoRAMoE, etc.)\n   - Failure detection/diagnosis in robotics\n   - Domain-specific adaptation / domain routing\n   - Test-time adaptation for robustness\n   - Robust visual policy transfer beyond FTM\n3. **Positioning and differentiation**: Does the paper adequately distinguish itself from SAFE (which also probes VLA internals for perturbation detection) and VLA-GSE (which also uses MoE routing)?\n4. **Contribution significance**: Are the three claimed contributions truly distinct, or is this one contribution (the system) presented as three?\n5. **Broader impact and limitations**: Are limitations honestly discussed? Is the simulation-only evaluation adequate for CoRL?\n6. **Writing quality and clarity**: Is the paper well-written? Is the narrative coherent?\n\nStructure your review as:\n### Summary\n### Strengths (S1, S2, ...)\n### Weaknesses (W1, W2, ...)\n### Questions for Authors (Q1, Q2, ...)\n### Missing References\n### Minor Issues\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Identify both genuine weaknesses and genuine strengths.","agentId":"a12a43b9fe4441923","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a thorough understanding of the paper and all project context. Let me produce the review.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper proposes Meta-Brain, a modular framework that diagnoses perturbation type from frozen VLA internal activations (via a linear probe on PaliGemma layer 6, F1=0.993) and routes to one of three type-specialized LoRA experts (Visual, Spatial, Physical) for targeted correction. The system achieves 78.4% on LIBERO-Plus with 6.1M trainable parameters, claiming +13.6% over universal LoRA and 19x fewer parameters than VLA-GSE (81.2%, 114M params). Cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA is provided.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n**S1: The core insight is well-motivated and empirically grounded.** The observation that VLA activations encode perturbation type at a single layer with near-perfect accuracy (F1=0.993) is a genuinely interesting empirical finding. The paper leverages this finding architecturally rather than just reporting it, which is the right approach. The \"close the loop\" framing -- SAFE detects but does not correct, Meta-Brain detects *and* corrects -- is a clean positioning.\n\n**S2: The experimental design is thorough within its scope.** The ablation ladder (Frozen -> Universal -> Meta-Brain -> Oracle) is well-constructed and the decomposition of the 26% total gain into routing contribution (13.6%, 52%) vs. LoRA contribution (11.2%, 43%) vs. headroom (1.2%, 5%) is informative. The oracle routing ablation (1.2% gap, p=0.14 n.s.) effectively demonstrates that the learned router is near-ceiling. The cross-evaluation matrix (Table 1) persuasively shows that expert corrections are type-specific and not simply additive robustness.\n\n**S3: The cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA strengthens the generality claim.** Demonstrating consistent gain patterns (+11.8% over universal LoRA) on a different VLA architecture (SigLIP + Llama-2-7B vs. PaliGemma + Gemma) goes beyond what most single-paper contributions provide. The fact that the best probe layer shifts (6 -> 8) while the overall mechanism transfers is a useful finding.\n\n**S4: The paper is well-structured and the narrative is coherent.** The paper flows logically from diagnosis to architecture to experiments. The 13 tables, while dense, each serves a clear purpose in the ablation hierarchy. The writing is generally clear and the claims are stated precisely.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n**W1 (Major): Limited novelty -- the contribution is a straightforward composition of well-known components.** The core architecture is: (1) a linear probe for classification (standard since [Alain & Bengio 2017]), (2) soft routing to multiple LoRA modules (standard MoE/MoLoRA pattern), and (3) independently-trained LoRA experts (standard PEFT). None of these components is novel individually, and their composition follows a natural design pattern that any practitioner familiar with both MoE and LoRA would arrive at. The specific instantiation -- routing by *perturbation type* rather than by task or token -- is a valid design choice but represents a modest conceptual step. The paper frames this as introducing a \"new routing dimension,\" but the routing mechanism itself (MLP on intermediate features -> softmax -> weighted sum of experts) is identical to standard MoE gating. The novelty lies in *what signal* drives routing (perturbation type), not in *how* routing is done. This is an application insight, not a methodological advance.\n\n**W2 (Major): Severely incomplete related work with missing citations for directly relevant methods.** The paper mentions MoIRA, CORAL, and LoRA-SP *by name* in Section 2 but provides no citations -- this is a significant omission for a published paper. More critically, the paper fails to cite or discuss several lines of work that are directly relevant:\n\n- **Mixture-of-LoRA-Experts literature**: LoRAMoE [Dou et al., 2024], MoLoRA [Zadouri et al., 2023], MixLoRA [Li et al., 2024], MOELoRA [Liu et al., 2023], and LoRA-Switch [Mok et al., 2024] all explore routing among multiple LoRA modules. Some of these use soft routing with gating functions very similar to PAR. The paper must explain how Meta-Brain differs from these methods beyond the choice of routing signal.\n\n- **Domain-conditional / context-conditional adaptation**: AdapterFusion [Pfeiffer et al., 2021], Poly [Ponti et al., 2023], and related work on composing adapters conditioned on input characteristics predate this work and share the same design pattern.\n\n- **Test-time adaptation / domain detection + correction**: Test-time training (TTT) [Sun et al., 2020] and TENT [Wang et al., 2021] perform online adaptation at test time based on detected distribution shifts. The relationship to these methods should be discussed -- Meta-Brain can be viewed as a discrete version of test-time adaptation where the \"adaptation\" is pre-computed.\n\n- **Failure diagnosis in robotics**: The robotics community has extensive work on failure detection, diagnosis, and recovery (e.g., Inceoglu et al., 2021; Di Lello et al., 2013). The paper should contextualize its perturbation diagnosis within this broader tradition.\n\n- **PaliGemma probing**: The paper should cite the original PaliGemma work [Beyer et al., 2024] separately from pi0, and the broader literature on probing representations for distribution shift detection (e.g., Guillory et al., 2021).\n\nWith only 8 references in total, the bibliography is remarkably thin for a venue like CoRL. A typical CoRL paper cites 30-50 works.\n\n**W3 (Major): Unfair parameter comparison with VLA-GSE masks the true competitive picture.** The headline claim -- \"19x fewer parameters than VLA-GSE\" -- compares across different backbones (pi0/PaliGemma vs. Qwen3-VL-4B). This is acknowledged in a footnote but nonetheless dominates the abstract, introduction, and conclusion. The comparison is misleading because: (a) the backbone sizes differ substantially (PaliGemma ~3B vs. Qwen3-VL-4B ~4B), making the ratio of trainable-to-total parameters different; (b) the absolute performance gap (78.4% vs. 81.2%) cannot be attributed to the method vs. the backbone; (c) the \"gain-over-frozen\" normalization used to make the comparison fairer (24.8 vs. 27.6) still compares gains on fundamentally different systems. A fair comparison would require running VLA-GSE's MoE routing on the same pi0 backbone, or running Meta-Brain on Qwen3-VL-4B. The OpenVLA cross-backbone result partially addresses generalizability but does not resolve the apples-to-oranges VLA-GSE comparison.\n\n**W4 (Major): The three \"contributions\" are really one contribution repackaged.** Contribution 1 (diagnosability) is a prerequisite observation. Contribution 2 (the architecture) is the system. Contribution 3 (parameter efficiency) is a consequence of the architecture design. These are not independent contributions -- they are the setup, the method, and a property of the method, respectively. Presenting them as three distinct contributions inflates the paper's apparent contribution scope. A more honest framing would be: \"We contribute Meta-Brain, a system that exploits the diagnosability of perturbation types (which we empirically establish) to achieve parameter-efficient routing of corrective LoRA experts.\"\n\n**W5 (Moderate): The routing setting is arguably too easy to be interesting.** The Phase 0 finding (F1=0.993, and F1=1.000 with first-token pooling at K=1) is presented as a strength, but it raises a question: if perturbation types are so trivially separable in the frozen representation space, does this mean that a universal LoRA with sufficient capacity (e.g., rank-48 instead of rank-16) could learn to handle all types without explicit routing? The paper compares Meta-Brain (3 x rank-16 = ~6M) against a rank-16 universal LoRA (~2M), but critically **never compares against a rank-48 universal LoRA (~6M)** that would match the total parameter budget. The claimed \"routing vs. uniform\" comparison confounds routing benefit with parameter count (6M vs. 2M). The paper acknowledges this in the imagined experiments (\"rank-48 universal LoRA should still saturate at ~67%\") but this experiment is not actually run. Without this control, Claim 2 is not properly established.\n\n**W6 (Moderate): Simulation-only evaluation is a limitation, though standard for the venue.** The paper correctly notes that SAFE, VLA-GSE, and RobustVLA are also sim-only. However, the \"perturbation type is known\" assumption is much stronger in simulation (where perturbation type is a controlled variable) than in reality (where multiple perturbations may co-occur, perturbation type may be ambiguous, or perturbations may be continuous rather than categorical). The 3-class taxonomy is a clean decomposition of LIBERO-Plus's controlled perturbation set, but it is unclear how this taxonomy would transfer to unstructured real-world perturbations where boundaries between \"visual\" and \"spatial\" are blurred.\n\n**W7 (Minor): The Correction Memory module adds complexity without sufficient justification.** CM adds 2.4% over Meta-Brain. While presented as \"optional,\" it occupies significant paper real estate (a dedicated subsection and table). The mechanism (FAISS k-NN retrieval modulating routing weights) is not novel and the gain is modest. This space would be better used for the missing parameter-matched baseline (W5) or expanded related work (W2).\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1:** Can you run a rank-48 universal LoRA (matching Meta-Brain's total ~6M parameter budget) as a baseline? Without this, the claim that routing outperforms uniform adaptation is confounded by parameter count.\n\n**Q2:** How does Meta-Brain handle mixed perturbations -- e.g., simultaneous camera angle change + layout shift? LIBERO-Plus applies perturbations individually; does the system degrade when multiple perturbation types co-occur? The soft routing mechanism could in principle handle this (assigning partial weight to multiple experts), but this is never tested.\n\n**Q3:** Why are MoIRA, CORAL, and LoRA-SP mentioned without citations? Are these concurrent or unpublished works? If they are published, citations are required. If they are internal names, they should not appear in the paper.\n\n**Q4:** The paper states the PAR uses \"mean-pooled layer-6 activations\" (Section 3.2), but the memory.md records a decision (D006) to switch to \"first-token pooling\" because it achieves F1=1.000 vs. mean pooling's 0.978. Which pooling method is actually used in the reported experiments? This inconsistency between the paper text and the project records needs clarification.\n\n**Q5:** The training procedure uses independently trained LoRA experts that are then frozen, with only the PAR fine-tuned end-to-end. Why not fine-tune the LoRA experts jointly with the router? What is the performance gap between this staged training and true end-to-end training?\n\n**Q6:** How does the system behave on clean (unperturbed) observations? If deployed on standard LIBERO (no perturbations), does Meta-Brain maintain the >90% baseline performance, or does routing introduce degradation?\n\n**Q7:** The \"random routing\" baseline from the experiment plan (plan_005) appears in the plan but not in the paper's ablation ladder (Table 3). Why was it removed? This is a critical control that distinguishes routing benefit from ensemble benefit.\n\n---\n\n### Missing References\n\nThe following categories of work should be cited and discussed:\n\n1. **MoIRA, CORAL, LoRA-SP** -- mentioned by name in the paper without citations. This is unacceptable in a published paper.\n\n2. **Mixture-of-LoRA literature**: LoRAMoE [Dou et al., 2024], MoLoRA [Zadouri et al., 2023], MOELoRA [Liu et al., 2023], MixLoRA [Li et al., 2024], LoRA-Switch [Mok et al., 2024].\n\n3. **Adapter composition/fusion**: AdapterFusion [Pfeiffer et al., 2021], Poly [Ponti et al., 2023], AdaMix [Wang et al., 2022].\n\n4. **Test-time adaptation**: TENT [Wang et al., 2021], TTT [Sun et al., 2020], or at minimum a sentence acknowledging this related direction.\n\n5. **PaliGemma**: [Beyer et al., 2024] -- the vision encoder being probed.\n\n6. **Representation probing foundations**: [Alain & Bengio, 2017] for linear probes; [Belinkov, 2022] for probing survey.\n\n7. **MoE fundamentals**: [Shazeer et al., 2017] or [Fedus et al., 2022] -- the gating mechanism in PAR is a direct descendant.\n\n8. **LIBERO base benchmark**: [Liu et al., 2024] -- the original LIBERO should be cited alongside LIBERO-Plus.\n\nThe current 8-reference bibliography is far below CoRL norms and creates the impression that the authors have not engaged with the broader literature.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. **Inconsistency in parameter counts**: The paper framing document states \"~6M\" while the LoRA injection verification shows 3 x 3.7M = 11.1M. The paper text uses 6.1M. These need reconciliation.\n\n2. **Table 6 (FTM comparison)**: The text states \"FTM achieves 71.5% average on visual subtypes\" but this value is not in the table -- the visual average from the table numbers is (70+76+72+68)/4 = 71.5, so it is correct but should be stated explicitly as a computed average.\n\n3. **Section 3.2 states PAR uses \"mean-pooled\" features** but project decision D006 switched to first-token pooling. The paper must reflect the actual implementation.\n\n4. **No architecture figure**: The paper describes the architecture in text only. A system diagram is essential for clarity and is listed as \"planned\" in the framing document but absent from the paper.\n\n5. **No t-SNE / feature visualization**: The paper claims perturbation types are separable in activation space but provides no visualization. A t-SNE plot of layer-6 activations colored by perturbation type would be compelling evidence and is standard for probing papers.\n\n6. **The LIBERO-Plus reference is cited as CVPR 2026** -- this would be a concurrent submission. If not yet published, this should be indicated as \"to appear\" or \"under review.\"\n\n7. **The VLA-GSE reference is cited as CoRL 2026** -- same venue as this submission. If this is a concurrent submission to the same venue, the comparison requires special care (blind review considerations, inability to verify their numbers independently).\n\n8. **All results appear to use even integer percentages** (42%, 54%, 72%, etc.), which is unusual for averaged results over 5 seeds. This suggests either rounding to suspicious precision or that the results are not from actual experiments.\n\n9. **The overall weighted average for Frozen pi0 (53.6%)** does not match a simple average of the six per-perturbation values (42+58+52+46+68+56)/6 = 53.67, which rounds to 53.7. This minor discrepancy should be clarified (perhaps due to unequal episode counts per perturbation type)."}],"totalDurationMs":150731,"totalTokens":80231,"totalToolUseCount":17,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3695,"cache_read_input_tokens":72691,"output_tokens":3844,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3695},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3844,"cache_read_input_tokens":72691,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3695,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3695,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":4,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":11}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DsaNakaRbPHG7MznBvNsve","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent. Both reviewers have submitted detailed independent reviews. Now I need to run the full roundtable process:\n\nStep 1: Independent reviews (done - R1 and R2 have submitted)\nStep 2: Cross-sharing (each reviewer sees the other's review and responds)\nStep 3: Convergence (discussion to align on key issues and scores)\nStep 4: Synthesis (combined report with final scores and recommendation)\n\nLet me now run Step 2: Cross-sharing. I'll send each reviewer the other's review and ask them to respond to the key points raised.\n\nActually, for efficiency, I can consolidate Step 2-4 into a synthesis since I have both reviews and can do the roundtable discussion myself as the Director/AC. \n\nNow I'm synthesizing both reviews to identify the convergent concerns. The key issues that both reviewers flagged are the missing parameter-matched baseline with a rank-48 universal LoRA around 6M parameters, the absent random routing baseline that was originally planned, and the unfair cross-backbone comparison for VLA-GSE.\n\nBoth also noted that all the reported results are suspiciously round numbers, there's inconsistency in pooling strategies between mean and first-token approaches, the evaluation is limited to simulation-only settings, the bibliography is sparse, and there's no clean baseline without perturbations. The CM module's improvements are modest, and R1 additionally raised concerns about how the overall success rate weights visual performance, missing latency analysis, no mixed perturbation evaluation, and unclear end-to-end training objectives.\n\nR2 emphasized the limited novelty—the approach is essentially a straightforward combination of existing components—and questioned whether the three claimed contributions actually constitute three separate advances. The routing task itself seems too easy, and the paper lacks an architecture diagram and t-SNE visualization.\n\nNow I'm moving through the cross-reviewer synthesis to identify convergence points and prepare the final scoring assessment. Both reviewers recognize the strong experimental breadth with many ablations and an interesting empirical finding about Phase 0 diagnosability, plus the paper is well-structured overall. However, they converge on critical gaps: missing parameter-matched and random routing baselines, unfair comparisons across different backbones, and fundamentally limited novelty in the approach.\n\nLooking at the additional concerns—the thin bibliography with only 8 references, suspicious result patterns where all scores are even integers, and evaluation limited to simulation on a single benchmark—these compound the baseline issues. Given CoRL's competitive acceptance rate around 30-35%, this paper would need to be quite strong to clear the bar, and the missing parameter-matched baseline is a fundamental problem that undermines the core claims. The sparse bibliography is also a red flag for a venue like CoRL.\n\nI'm settling on rough scores: R1 around 5-6 on methods and experiments (marginally below to marginally above, but leaning toward below because of those critical missing baselines), and R2 around 4-5 on novelty and related work (insufficient novelty combined with inadequate bibliography coverage). Now I'll synthesize this into the full roundtable report.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12487,"cache_read_input_tokens":69334,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12487},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f5e0932-32f2-4b4b-9817-0d2ade2fda2a","uuid":"fee1e0cd-8990-468a-863e-c4398c4132f7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DsaNakaRbPHG7MznBvNsve","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both reviewers have submitted independent reviews. Now proceeding with the roundtable: Step 2 (cross-sharing), Step 3 (convergence), Step 4 (synthesis with final scores).\n\nLet me run the cross-sharing step where each reviewer responds to the other's key points."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12487,"cache_read_input_tokens":69334,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12487},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f5e0932-32f2-4b4b-9817-0d2ade2fda2a","uuid":"112f398b-79c1-46e7-8a41-42f2d3818fcf"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DsaNakaRbPHG7MznBvNsve","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CQSAAuknMmVcs6rAKpCSTu","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Roundtable Step 2-3: Cross-sharing and convergence","prompt":"You are the Area Chair (AC) mediating a roundtable discussion between two reviewers (R1 and R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission titled \"Meta-Brain: Perturbation-Diagnostic Routing of LoRA Experts for Parameter-Efficient VLA Robustness.\"\n\n## Venue\nCoRL 2026, 1-10 scale (7=accept, 5=borderline), ~30-35% acceptance rate.\n\n## R1 Review (Methods + Experiments)\n\n### Strengths\nS1: Well-motivated architecture with clear empirical grounding (Phase 0 F1=0.993)\nS2: Thorough ablation ladder with appropriate stats (5 seeds, p<0.001)\nS3: Expert cross-evaluation matrix provides strong specialization evidence\nS4: Comprehensive ablation coverage (11 experiments, 13 tables)\nS5: Layer placement ablation provides mechanistic insight (V-early, S-mid, P-late)\nS6: Cross-backbone validation on OpenVLA\n\n### Weaknesses\nW1 (Major): Parameter comparison unfair — Meta-Brain (6M) vs Universal LoRA (2M) is 3x. Missing rank-48 universal LoRA control.\nW2 (Major): Random routing baseline from plan_005 missing — needed to isolate routing from ensemble effect.\nW3 (Major): Cross-backbone VLA-GSE comparison confounded (different backbone: pi0 vs Qwen3-VL-4B). Headline \"19x fewer params\" is overstated.\nW4 (Moderate): All success rates are even integers (multiples of 2.0). Suspicious — suggests very low episode counts or rounding.\nW5 (Moderate): Paper says \"mean-pooled\" but project decision log says \"first-token pooling\" (F1=1.000 vs 0.978). Inconsistency.\nW6 (Moderate): Single benchmark (LIBERO-Plus), sim-only.\nW7 (Minor): CM adds complexity for modest +2.4% gain.\nW8 (Minor): \"Overall\" success rate gives 4/6 weight to visual types.\n\n### Missing Experiments\nM1 (Critical): Parameter-matched universal LoRA (rank-48, ~6M)\nM2 (Critical): Random routing baseline\nM3 (Important): Clean/unperturbed LIBERO evaluation\nM4 (Important): Latency/throughput analysis\nM5 (Desirable): Mixed/compound perturbation evaluation\nM6 (Desirable): Per-seed variance for per-perturbation tables\n\n### Key Minor Issues\n- Incomplete bibliography (MoIRA, CORAL, LoRA-SP mentioned without citations)\n- E2E training objective unspecified (how does gradient flow through soft routing?)\n- Cross-type degradation claim misleading (-20% is not vs frozen but vs own-type)\n- Unclear evaluation episode count\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review (Novelty + Related Work)\n\n### Strengths\nS1: Core insight well-motivated (SAFE detects but doesn't correct; Meta-Brain does both)\nS2: Thorough experimental design within scope (ablation ladder decomposition is informative)\nS3: Cross-backbone validation strengthens generality claim\nS4: Well-structured narrative\n\n### Weaknesses\nW1 (Major): Limited novelty — straightforward composition of known components (linear probe + MoE routing + LoRA). \"New routing dimension\" (perturbation type) is an application insight, not methodological advance.\nW2 (Major): Severely incomplete related work. Only 8 references total. Missing: LoRAMoE, MoLoRA, MixLoRA, MOELoRA, LoRA-Switch, AdapterFusion, Poly, TENT/TTT, PaliGemma, Alain & Bengio probing, MoE fundamentals (Shazeer). MoIRA/CORAL/LoRA-SP mentioned by name without citations.\nW3 (Major): Cross-backbone VLA-GSE comparison confounded (same as R1-W3).\nW4 (Major): Three \"contributions\" are really one contribution repackaged (observation + system + consequence).\nW5 (Moderate): Routing setting too easy (F1=0.993). Missing rank-48 universal baseline to test if sufficient capacity eliminates need for routing.\nW6 (Moderate): Sim-only. 3-class taxonomy designed for LIBERO-Plus's controlled perturbation set.\nW7 (Minor): CM adds complexity for modest gain (same as R1-W7).\n\n### Missing References\n- MoIRA, CORAL, LoRA-SP (mentioned without citations)\n- LoRAMoE, MoLoRA, MixLoRA, MOELoRA, LoRA-Switch\n- AdapterFusion, Poly, AdaMix\n- TENT, TTT\n- PaliGemma [Beyer et al. 2024]\n- Linear probing foundations [Alain & Bengio 2017]\n- MoE fundamentals [Shazeer et al. 2017]\n- Original LIBERO benchmark\n\n### Key Minor Issues\n- No architecture figure\n- No t-SNE visualization of perturbation clusters\n- Concurrent submission citations (LIBERO-Plus CVPR 2026, VLA-GSE CoRL 2026)\n- All results suspiciously even integers\n- Overall average discrepancy (53.67 rounds to 53.7, not 53.6)\n\n---\n\n## Your Task\n\nPerform Steps 2 and 3 of the roundtable:\n\n**Step 2 (Cross-sharing):** For each reviewer, identify:\n- Points from the other reviewer that they would likely agree with\n- Points they would likely push back on\n- New insights that emerge from comparing both reviews\n\n**Step 3 (Convergence):** Synthesize into:\n1. **Agreed strengths** (both reviewers concur)\n2. **Agreed weaknesses** (both reviewers concur)\n3. **Disputed points** (reviewers disagree — state both sides)\n4. **Severity assessment**: For each agreed weakness, assess whether it is addressable in a revision or fundamental\n5. **Preliminary score range**: Based on the convergence, what score range (on 1-10) does each reviewer likely gravitate toward? Provide reasoning.\n\nBe specific and analytical. Don't just list — synthesize and judge."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12487,"cache_read_input_tokens":69334,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12487},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f5e0932-32f2-4b4b-9817-0d2ade2fda2a","uuid":"4f64cf61-10c1-40e7-8fac-fe4eefd4da8c"}
